# Untitled

Heterogeneous Nanosized Metal (Metallic Compound) @ Metal-Organic Framework Composites: Recent Advances in the Preparation and Applications
Metal-organic framework (MOF) composites based on MOFs-wrapped metals or metallic compounds (denoted as M/MC@MOFs) are an important class of materials that integrate M/MCs and MOFs. MOFs can fix and confine M/MCs to regulate their size, prevent uncontrollable aggregation, and enhance the exposure and ordered arrangement of active sites. In turn, M/MCs support the MOFs to enhance their stability and realize more targeted properties. In this review, the features and application scope of different M/MC@MOF synthesis methods are explored, considering the influential factors and regulation modes of the M/MC–MOFs integration. Additionally, the performances of the M/MC@MOF composites are comprehensively discussed, and the underlying mechanisms are clarified. The performance of M/MC@MOFs is dependent on the orderly synergy between the MOFs and M/MCs. MOFs can sieve and transfer specific molecules to the M/MC surface, whereas the robust interface provides new charge/energy-transfer pathways, which enhance the activity and selectivity of the composites for diverse molecules. Furthermore, the challenges facing this field are analyzed, and the recommendations for advancing the development of M/MC@MOF composites are presented.
## 1. Introduction
The combination of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and metals or metallic compounds (denoted as M/MCs) has been a worthwhile topic of research in recent years. M/MCs exhibit particularly high activities and unique physical and chemical properties.[1–5] The performance of M/MCs is highly size dependent, with small M/MC particles having a large specific surface area often exhibiting desirable characteristics. M/MCs are typically fixed and stabilized to prevent them from agglomerating, aid them in exhibiting more impressive properties, and help in achieving prolong activity. MOFs are a type of classical 3D crystalline ordered porous coordination polymers,[6–10] whose
pore size and cavity environment can be optimized by designing the structural characteristics of ligands and the coordination mode of the metal–metal clusters.[11–17] Compositing M/MCs and MOFs not only resolves the limitations of MOFs such as inferior solvent resistance,[18] thermal stability, degree of formability,[19] and reproducibility,[20] but also overcomes the disadvantages of M/MCs such as easy aggregation, poor corrosion resistance, and disordered sites arrangement, which enhance the properties of the materials by the combination form of M/MC and MOFs.[21–24]
The most popular mode of combining M/MCs and MOFs is introducing M/MCs into MOFs (M/MC@MOFs). Through space-limiting effects, MOFs can restrict and fix M/MCs to regulate their size, prevent uncontrollable migration, and promote the ordered arrangement of active M/MCs. In M/MC@MOFs, the MOFs and M/MCs exhibit a highly ordered synergy,
and the internal M/MC is significantly dependent on the chemical properties of the external MOFs. As the first screening barrier, the MOFs can selectively adsorb and transfer specific substances to the M/MC surface, enhancing the chemical selectivity. Moreover, the high mechanical stability of the MOF increases the service life of the internal M/MCs. In turn, the M/MC bolsters the external MOF—improving its stability—and may flexibly guide the formation of MOFs with a denser interface. Additionally, the robust-interface-induced interactions can provide benefits such as creating new charge/energy-transfer pathways and increasing the active site exposure,[25] which boosts the utilization of the inherent characteristics of the MOF and M/MC,[26–29] thereby amplifying the performance. Consequently, M/MC@MOF composites have significant potential in fields such as anti-counterfeiting, electronics,[30] catalysis,[31,32] and environmental governance.
Recent progress in research on M/MC@MOF composites is comprehensively reviewed herein. Aspects of the frequently used synthesis methods including the advantages, disadvantages, and application scope are summarized. The modes for regulating the amount and dispersion of M/MCs, and the morphology, thickness, and growth direction of MOFs are also discussed. Subsequently, the excellent properties of M/MC@MOFs are
classified and summarized, with emphasis on the performance tuning of the composites and the mechanisms underlying the synergy between the internal M/MC and external MOF. In particular, attention is paid to the M/MC–MOF synergy that relies on the orderly coordination and dense interface, which is key to the M/MC@MOFs outperforming their MOF and M/MC components. This review, which provides useful reference data, is hoped to arouse the interest of researchers investigating M/MC@MOF composites. We recommend further exploring the articles cited herein to acquire more detailed information on M/MC@MOFs.
## 2. Preparation of Heterogeneous M/MC@MOF Composites
The open-hole network feature,[33] molecular scaffold function,[34] and molecular grasping ability of MOFs facilitate the capture of M/MCs.[28,35,36] M/MC@MOFs can be obtained using liquid-, solid-, or gas-phase synthesis methods (Figure 1).[37] Liquid-phase synthesis methods (including template synthesis, seed-mediated growth, one-pot method, in-situ pyrolysis,[38] and adsorption method) are highly versatile; solid-state synthesis schemes (including solvent-free and microsolvent-assisted methods) typically fall under green chemistry; whereas gas-phase
synthesis procedures (including atomic layer deposition (ALD) and chemical vapor deposition (CVD)) involve vapor deposition technologies.
## 2.1. Liquid-Phase Synthesis
Liquid-phase synthesis can be achieved through two routes depending on the sequence of events between MOF nucleation and M/MC–MOF assembly. If the M/MC is introduced in conjunction with the gradual nucleation of the MOF, synthesis schemes such as template synthesis, seed-mediated growth, the one-pot method, and in situ pyrolysis are employed. However, if the M/MC is introduced after the MOF synthesis, the adsorption method is adopted.
## 2.1.1. Template Synthesis
Certain M/MCs can guide the MOF growth during their introduction; this process is termed as template synthesis. Generally, the template M/MC—which acts as the core—must have sufficient acting force to prevent the self-crystallization of the MOF, otherwise, the phase shift of MOFs will occur.[39]
The self-sacrificial strategy is a typical template-based synthesis approach. For instance, the sacrificial template \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})\mathrm{PO}_{4}\) acted as a \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) source to compound with trimesic acid, thus forming \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\).[40] The self-sacrificial M/MC that acts as a template is generally isotropic, affects only the M/MC distribution, and determines the surface morphology and thickness of the MOF without affecting its microstructure and crystal growth direction. For example, as a sacrificial template, \(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{ZnO}\) controlled the dispersion of Pd and \(95~\mathrm{nm}\) thick ZIF-8,[41] whereas \(\mathrm{[Cu_{2.5}S O_{4}(O H)_{3}{\cdot}2H_{2}O]}\) realized a dense and uniform distribution of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) on its surface.[42] Furthermore, the addition of another M/MC to the self-sacrificial-M/MC-containing solution is a reliable approach to realizing multiM/MCs-package of MOFs. For example, both \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and Pt were introduced into MIL-101(Fe) by mixing Pt nanoparticles into the original reaction system with \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) acting as the self-sacrificial core (Figure 2a).[43] The self-sacrificial strategy can also prevent the self-deposition of MOFs in solution, promote the high uniformity of MOFs, and enable high adhesion to the heterogeneous interface, effectively stabilizing the composites and averting the performance attenuation.[44] For example, Gong and co-workers compared the growth of nickel-foam@NiFe-MOF (NF@NiFe-MOF) and NF/NiFe-MOF formed using self-sacrificial and non
sacrificial strategies, respectively.[45] \(\mathrm{NF}@\mathrm{NiFe-MOF}\) existed as 2D ultrathin nanosheets, whereas \(\mathrm{NF}/\mathrm{NiFe-MOF}\) had a layered nanosheet structure. Moreover, \(\mathrm{NF}@\mathrm{NiFe-MOF}\) had a higher dispersion of NiFe-MOF on the NF surface than that in NF/NiFe-MOF.
By regulating the sacrificial velocity of the self-sacrificial template and the coordinated rate of MOFs, the dense growth of MOFs on the M/MC surface can be controlled. Once the dissolved speed of M/MC is too fast, too many metal ions are easy to coordinate with ligands to generate many MOFs and cause them uncontrollably deposit in the solution. Jiang and co-workers precisely controlled the etching speed of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) by adjusting the reaction time, temperature, and the feedstock ratio, and ensured that the sacrificial speed of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was identical to the assembly time of the MOF, which helped yield a perfectly heterogeneous \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\) composite with \(220\;\mathrm{nm}\) thick HKUST-1 (Figure 2b).[46] Owing to the controllable thickness and robust connection, the collaborative effects of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and HKUST-1 in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\) were superior to those in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{HKUST-1}\), which led to \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\) exhibiting higher selectivity and cyclicity (100% conversion from nitrobenzene to \(N\)-benzylideneaniline) than those of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{HKUST-1}\). Differently shaped MOFs can be produced by designing M/MCs with unique
configurations (including flower-like, rod-like, sheet-like, and spherical structures). For instance, Huo and co-workers assembled core–shell-structured metal@metal oxide as a sacrificial template, which helped form \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{MOF}\) with a heterogeneous petalous structure upon the gradual sacrifice of the metal oxide in the metal@metal oxide system.[47]
## 2.1.2. Seed-Mediated Growth
This technique is a custom-surface-based chemical synthesis method that essentially involves nucleation control. Seed-mediated growth, which was originally performed to adjust the size and morphology of metal nanoparticles,[48,49] was subsequently adopted to synthesize \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) owing to its mediating role. Moreover, in this approach, the MOF crystallization bypasses the dynamic equilibration of dissociation and growth, which is the most energy-intensive process of the MOF crystal cluster formation. Specifically, the M/MC—which acts as a seed—is placed in the MOF precursor solution, and the ligands interactively adsorb onto the M/MC surface to yield ligand@M/MC, which gradually react with the metal ions to stimulate the MOF growth. For instance, Au nanoclusters (AuNCs) were added to the ZIF-8 precursor solution to mediate the production of \(\mathrm{AuNC@ZIF-8}\) depending on the AuNC–ligand interactions.[50]
M/MCs with more functionalities can cooperatively interact with MOFs to yield \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) with improved performance.[51] For instance, functionalized magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) was used as a seed to synthesize MOFs, which were used to directly assemble magnetically reinforced \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL-100}(\mathrm{Fe})\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{EuMOF}\).[52,53] Seed-mediated growth is currently considered a classic growth mode for \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) composites. This method is relatively simple, and the MOFs generally have no directional selectivity on the M/MC surface.[54] For example, \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO-66-X}\) \((\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{H},\ \mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H},\ \mathrm{or}\ \mathrm{NH}_{2})\) was synthesized by mixing Pt seeds and the MOF precursor solution.[55] Many \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) —such as heterogenous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{Cu}\)
MOF, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\), and \(\mathrm{NaYbF}_{4}:\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{MIL-125}\) —can be synthesized in this manner.[56,57]
The surface engineering of seeds predominantly influences the MOF growth. Surface linkers, which modify the M/MC seeds, directly affect the seed/MOF-precursor interactions, enabling precise MOF growth in the desired manner.[58,59] Commonly used surface linkers with numerous active sites include polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), cetyltrimethyl ammonium bromide, and poly(ethylene glycol). For example, PVP was grafted onto the surface of \(\mathrm{Gd}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}:\mathrm{Yb},\mathrm{Er}\) to promote the growth of UiO\(\mathrm{66-NH}_{2}\).[60] Moreover, Farha and co-workers selected thiolate polyethylene glycol as the surface linker for gold nanorods (AuNRs) (Figure 3a), because its hard and soft atoms of the linkers could interact with the NU-901 precursor, directly producing heterogeneous \(\mathrm{AuNR}@\mathrm{NU-901}\) \((>\!99\%\) yield) with an adequate surface morphology and a clear interface.[61] The clear, robust interface enabled remarkable AuNR-NU-901 synergy, which led to \(\mathrm{AuNR}@\mathrm{NU-901}\) selectively absorbing or blocking specific molecules to achieve the end-sensing functionality of the M/MC. Surface linkers can also guide the growth direction of the MOFs to yield \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) with different performances. Li and coworkers employed lanthanide-doped upconversion nanoparticles (UCNPs) as seeds to investigate the mediating effect of different modified linkers for a porphyrinic MOF.[62] Citric acid (CA) and PVP were used to modify the UCNPs, and the modified seeds were added to the porphyrinic MOF precursor solution (Figure 3b). The CA-UCNPs induced the growth of an \(\approx8\,\mathrm{nm}\) thick MOF on the surface, whereas the PVP-UCNPs only synthesized a heterodimer rather than a \(\mathrm{UCNP@MOF}\) system. Owing to the different growth directions, the contact and synergy between the UCNPs and MOFs varied, which led to the \(\mathrm{UCNP@MOF}\) exhibiting a more efficient energy transfer than that of the heterodimer. Furthermore, upon exposure to near-infrared light irradiation, the \(\mathrm{UCNP@MOF}\) system triggered the production of cytotoxic reactive oxygen species, confirming its effectiveness as a photodynamic therapeutic agent. Overall, these studies suggest that the surface engineering of M/MCs can regulate the growth morphology and direction of heterogeneous MOFs. Furthermore, aspects of the seeds including
morphology, size, and number can influence the MOF size. For instance, Farha and co-workers verified that the size of NU-901 gradually decreased with increasing AuNR seed concentration.[61] Moreover, the seed size has been found to affect the growth of ZIF-class composites.[63] Additionally, for seeds with a definite morphology, the different reaction times of the seeds and MOF precursors can also regulate the thickness of the MOF on the M/MC seed surfaces.
## 2.1.3. One-Pot Method
The synthesis method can reduce the surface-matching energy demand of heterogeneous M/MC and MOFs. There are two synthesis pathways, and the first approach involves mixing the M/MC and MOF precursors, production of the M/MC, and then the layer-by-layer growth of the MOF on the M/MC surface. For example, Zhang and co-workers placed a ligand, \( \mathrm{ZnO} \), and \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4} \) in a reaction bottle and then produced Pd, around which ZIF-8 formed gradually, eventually producing \( \mathrm{Pd/ZnO@ZIF-8} \).[64] Similarly, in the one-pot reaction between \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6} \) and the precursors of DUT-5, UiO-66, and MOF-253, \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{4} \) generated Pt nanoparticles, whose surfaces were subsequently attached by the formed MOFs, yielding heterogenous \( \mathrm{Pt@MOFs} \).[65] The second approach is adopted if the precursors of M/MC cannot form preferentially M/MC in situ that are further wrapped by the formed MOFs. Here, the M/MC precursors are first introduced into the MOF using the one-pot method and processed further to obtain M/MC@MOFs. Systems such as \( \mathrm{Pd@MOF} \), \( \mathrm{Pt@MOF} \), and \( \mathrm{Au@MOF} \) have been realized by first introducing precursors such as \( \mathrm{PdCl}_{4}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{AuCl}_{4}^{+} \), and \( \mathrm{PtCl}_{6}^{2+} \) into the MOFs, and then adding a reductant.[66–68] This approach is typically employed to synthesize M@MOFs. Compared to the first approach, this synthesis strategy is more directional and is more suitable for simultaneously introducing multiple metals (for example, the concurrent encapsulation of Pd and Ni into UiO-67).[69]
## 2.1.4. In Situ Pyrolysis
This method is employed to synthesize defective heterogenous \( \mathrm{M/MC@MOFs} \), and its most noteworthy feature is the omission of additional M/MC precursors. At high temperatures, the MOF is partially etched and decomposes to an \( \mathrm{M/MC} \), and the MOF entirely encages the \( \mathrm{M/MC} \) to form \( \mathrm{M/MC@MOFs} \). Xiao and coworkers heated UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) at \( 200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 1\,\mathrm{h} \), leading to the partial collapse and decomposition of the MOF into \( \mathrm{ZrO}_{2} \), which was immediately enclosed by UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \), yielding \( \mathrm{ZrO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO-66-} \) \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \).[70] Therefore, to obtain \( \mathrm{M@MOFs} \), reductants should participate in the pyrolysis to change the valence state of the central metals. For instance, the hydroquinone ligand was used as a reductant when heating Ni-MOF-74.[71] With increasing temperature, an electron from the ligand was transferred to \( \mathrm{Ni}^{2+} \) and certain coordination bonds were ruptured, resulting in Ni nanoparticles encaged by Ni-MOF-74. Owing to the randomly dispersed reduction of \( \mathrm{Ni}^{2+} \), Ni particles that were only a few nanometers in size were produced. Ni particles as small as \( 2.3\;\mathrm{nm} \) were produced upon heating at \( 300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \), and \( \mathrm{Ni@Ni-MOF-74} \) exhibited antiferromagnetic as well as superparamagnetic behavior, which were highly related to the size. Therefore, the size of
the metals and the relative metal/MOF contents can be varied by controlling the reduction temperature, reaction time, and reductant dosage. Wen and co-workers synthesized \( \mathrm{Ni@Ni-MOF} \) in this manner (Figure 4a).[72] By adding the reductant at different concentrations, they reduced the MOF to \( \mathrm{Ni@Ni-MOF-X} \) with different Ni/Ni-MOF ratios. \( \mathrm{Co@NMOF-Co} \) has also been assembled similarly.
To expand the application range of this method, Wu and coworkers developed a new approach to produce \( \mathrm{M@MOF} \) composites with diverse metals, and to adequately regulate the size and distribution of the metals.[73] First, the homogeneously distributed "dual-MOF" material HKUST-1/ZIF-8 was synthesized (Figure 4b). Owing to the different pyrolysis temperatures of HKUST-1 and ZIF-8, HKUST-1 first decomposed at temperatures below \( 350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) to form \( \mathrm{CuNPs} \), which were uniformly encapsulated by ZIF-8—which preserved its skeleton—yielding \( \mathrm{Cu@ZIF-8} \). Surprisingly, the CuNP size decreased with increasing proportion of ZIF-8 in HKUST-1/ZIF-8. Notably, \( \mathrm{Cu@ZIF-8} \) combined the molecule-sieving properties of ZIF-8 and the size-tunable characteristics of the CuNPs to convert alkynes into olefins with high activity and selectivity.
In theory, \( \mathrm{MC@MOFs} \) can also be synthesized by in-situ pyrolysis of dual-MOF-type MOF/MOFs. Although few \( \mathrm{M/MC@MOFs} \) have been synthesized using this method owing to the uncontrollable rupture of coordination bonds, in-situ pyrolysis can still provide valuable reference data for producing heterogeneous \( \mathrm{M/MC@MOF} \) composites.
## 2.1.5. Adsorption Method
This method can be implemented through two approaches: direct adsorption (one-step or multistep impregnation) and adsorption–reduction. Direct-adsorption-based synthesis requires strong forces between the MOFs, \( \mathrm{M/MCs} \), and their precursors—such as interaction sites and electric charge—which significantly affect the accessibility of \( \mathrm{M/MC@MOFs} \).[74] For example, Szilágyi and co-workers explored the ability of UiO-66 with different functional groups (–Cl, –OH, and –Br) to capture Pd nanoclusters.[75] Compared to the –Br sites, which exhibited high Pd adsorption capacity, the –Cl and –OH sites exhibited negligible Pd capturing ability, and the weak acting force of \( (\mathrm{Cl})_{2} \)-UiO-66 was derived only from the attractive tendency of UiO-66, which was insufficient for overcoming the sintering-associated autonucleation of Pd. The high enthalpy of adsorption for Pd and the small difference in adsorption enthalpy between the Pd dimer and the independent Pd atoms on Br-UiO-66 led to the Br-UiO-66 cavity exhibiting an optimal Pd immobilization rate. Therefore, owing to the interactions of the functional sites on the MOF, the states of metals may change when entering the MOF, and the difference between the overall adsorption enthalpy and the adsorption enthalpy of molecules induces the MOF to exhibit diverse metal-embedding abilities.[76] Moreover, the entry rate and loading can be controlled using the reaction temperature, impregnation time, and concentrations of the \( \mathrm{M/MCs} \) and their precursors. For example, different amounts of CdS were introduced into MOF-808, NU-1000, PCN-222, and MIL-53(Fe) by immersing the MOFs into a
cadmium acetate dihydrate/thioacetamide solution of different concentrations.[77,78]
Adsorption–reduction is a classic approach to synthesize \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{MOFs}\) by introducing metals into MOFs.[79,80] Using this method, Yang and co-workers assembled ZIF-8@ZIF-67, mixed it with \(\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\) to adsorb \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\)into the MOF, and then reduced \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67\) to obtain heterogeneous \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67\).[81] Controlling the degree of reduction can permit the loading of multi-valent metals. For instance, \(\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})\) synthesized using the adsorption method was placed in ascorbic acid under \(\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for a redox reaction, which generated \(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}/\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})\) with dual-valent metals (Figure 5a).[82] The amounts of the metals in MOFs can also be controlled by changing the concentration of the metal precursors. For example, \(\mathrm{Pb}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) with different Pd contents and \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{M}-\) BTC with different MOF metal centers have been prepared.[83,84] Similarly, the amounts of different metals in ZIF-67 and Cu-benzene dicarboxylic acid (BDC) have been adjusted by control
ling the corresponding metal precursors.[85,86] The most prominent advantage of adsorption–reduction is the design accessibility and predictability of incorporating metals into MOFs. Typically, a certain amount of metal precursors can be fixed to one position by situating appropriate organic linkers at specific locations, which can yield accurately regulated \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{MOFs}\) . Farha et al. grafted 4-carboxy-phenylacetylene onto the Zr cluster of NU1000, and then add the metal precursor Au–triethylphosphine \(\left(\mathrm{PET}_{3}\right)\) into the system, which replaced the acetylene proton connected to the Zr cluster and helped install \(\mathrm{Au-PEt}_{3}^{+}\)at a specific position. After adding \(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}\) , \(\mathrm{Au}^{+}\)was reduced to \(\mathrm{Au}^{0}\) , which subsequently aggregated and migrated directionally. Finally, Au was uniformly distributed in the triangular cavity of NU-1000, with the hexagonal cavity of the MOF completely exposed and unoccupied (Figure 5b). Owing to the dispersion and stabilization of the AuNPs in the triangular pore, \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NU}{-}1000\) exhibited high activity and stability in the condensed-phase hydrogenation catalysis of 4-nitrophenol to 4-aminophenol.[87]
The introduction of different metals into a single MOF to form \(\mathrm{M}/\mathrm{M}^{\prime}@\mathrm{MOF}\) systems can be readily achieved by simultaneously adsorbing diverse metallic precursors and then performing reduction.[88] For example, \(\mathrm{Pt}_{x}\mathrm{Cu}_{y}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\) systems with different \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Cu}\) loading proportions have been synthesized based on the adsorption of the \(\mathrm{H}_{x}\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) precursors by MIL-101.[89] Kim and co-workers obtained \(\mathrm{PtRu}@\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) by immersing \(\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) in a mixed solution of \(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}\) and then adding \(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}\).[90] \(\mathrm{PdAu}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})\) and \(\mathrm{AgPd}@\mathrm{Zr}-\) MOF have been produced similarly.[91,92] Additionally, continuous adsorption–reduction of different metallic precursors can be performed to synthesize \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{M}^{\prime}@\mathrm{MOFs}\), which is another noteworthy feature of adsorption–reduction. For example, \(\mathrm{Xu}\) and co-workers introduced two metals—Au and Ag—into MOFs using this process for the first time.[93] Briefly, ZIF-8 was soaked in \(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) and reduced to obtain \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\), which was further immersed in \(\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\) and reduced to yield \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\). \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption experiments confirmed the gradual deposition of \(\mathrm{Au}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag}\) in ZIF-8. Surprisingly, \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) was obtained by modifying the adsorption–reduction sequence of ZIF-8 for \(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\). Moreover, altering the concentrations of the \(\mathrm{Ag}\) and \(\mathrm{Au}\) precursors varied the \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{Au}\) composition ratio of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) and \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\). This study verified that the adsorption–reduction method could adequately control the distribution and content of the metals in MOFs. \(\mathrm{M}/\mathrm{M}^{\prime}@\mathrm{MOF}\) and \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{M}^{\prime}@\mathrm{MOF}\) systems have distinct interfaces and uniformly dispersed components, but cannot be readily formed by direct adsorption. Continuous deposition by adsorption–reduction adequately compensates for the deficiency of the direct adsorption method for metals.
## 2.1.6. Summary
Liquid-phase synthesis methods have been extensively used to produce \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\). As shown in Table 1, in template syn
thesis, the \(\mathrm{M/MC}\) template guides the morphology, thickness, growth direction, and compactness of the MOF on the \(\mathrm{M/MC}\). In seed-mediated growth—which is the most classic \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOF}\) synthesis approach—the \(\mathrm{M/MC}\) seeds control the size and morphology of the materials. However, the MOF may be unevenly distributed over the \(\mathrm{M/MC}\) surface or even precipitate in solution, which is the most common problem of this synthesis method. The one-pot method is simple and easy to operate, but faces difficulty in terms of accurately regulating the MOF and \(\mathrm{M/MC}\) contents. This issue can be overcome using the adsorption method, whose most prominent advantage is the controllability of the amount and type of the \(\mathrm{M/MC}\). Moreover, this method can achieve multimetal encapsulation with MOFs by regulating the type of the metal precursors. In contrast to the aforementioned approaches, in situ pyrolysis does not require additional reactants, but depends only on the rupture of coordination bonds in the MOF. The prominent advantage of this method is the random and uniform dispersion of the \(\mathrm{M/MC}\) in the MOF. Nevertheless, its applicability is limited owing to the uncontrolled collapse of the MOF. Essentially, each synthesis method has its own unique characteristics and advantages, along with certain limitations. Fortunately, these methods are complementary and can act as a technical reference for developing most \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\).
## 2.2. Solid-State Synthesis
The synthesis method, which is considered relevant to green chemistry,[94–98] typically involves three continuous steps: 1) phase rebuilding, which is crucial because reactants must migrate appropriately to form crystal-correlated surface features; 2) phase transformation due to the discontinuous lattice formation of products; and 3) crystal disintegration of the reactants with the formation of a new surface, owing to the different crystal structures of the reactants and products. The solid-state reaction will
cease if these three steps are discontinuous or one of the steps is terminated. Therefore, solid-state synthesis methods usually require external mechanical forces such as mechanical pressure and grinding to create new contacts, which can help implement the phase contact, phase transformation, and new-phase formation stages.[99]
## 2.2.1. Solvent-Free Solid-State Reaction
Solvent-free mechanical grinding and mechanical pressurization are the two main methods of forming M/MC@MOF composites via the solvent-free solid-state reaction. The most prominent characteristics of mechanical grinding are its simplicity, straightforward operation, and amenability to large-scale composite production.[100] The use of short reaction times is another prominent advantage of mechanical grinding, highlighting the significantly reduced atmospheric exposure of the introduced M/MC, which mitigates the damage induced by external environments to the activity of the M/MC.[101–103] Moreover, mechanical grinding is a typical dynamic process, which suggests that the size of the M/MC@MOF composite particles under dynamic conditions is generally small.[104]
Tanaka et al. employed solvent-free grinding to perform in-situ synthesis of ZnO@ZIF-8.[105] The mechanical-grinding-associated kinetic energy induced high-pressure local heating and melting, which drove the solid surface reaction of the reactants, leading to a reduction in the ZnO particle size, particle
Tanaka et al. employed solvent-free grinding to perform in-situ synthesis of ZnO@ZIF-8.[105] The mechanical-grinding-associated kinetic energy induced high-pressure local heating and melting, which drove the solid surface reaction of the reactants, leading to a reduction in the ZnO particle size, particle collisions, and lattice dislocations. ZnO@ZIF-8 was completely formed as ZnO gradually transformed into ZIF-8. Notably, the production of the relatively complete composites relied largely on the gradual sacrificial transformation of ZnO into MOFs. In fact, the introduction of sacrificial templates in the solid-state synthesis process can effectively control the uniformity of the packing. For instance, by prepositioning Pd on the ZnO sacrificial template, Xiao and co-workers achieved controllable growth of ZIF-8 around a Pd core and uniform dispersion of Pd in ZIF-8 (Figure 6).[106] Apart from the direct in situ synthesis, mechanical grinding can also realize the post-synthesis introduction of MOFs for M/MCs. Xu and co-workers initially implanted the Au precursor Me2Au(acac) into ZIF-8 through solvent-free grinding, and then used H2 as the reductant to produce the Au@ZIF-8 composite.[107] The Au nanoparticle content of the ZIF-8 composite was controlled by changing the Au precursor/ZIF-8 ratio.
Unlike solvent-free mechanical grinding, solvent-free mechanical pressurization is simply a static reaction. Under mechanical pressure, only the size, shape, and surface properties of reactants can be changed, and the contact between MOF precursors will not vary dynamically. Therefore, the main factor for achieving M/MC@MOF synthesis is typically the chemical action (such as hydrogen bonding) between the M/MC and ligands. Coronas and co-workers employed high pressures to promote the solid-state reaction between ZnO and 2-methylimidazole, forming ZnO@ZIF within 2 min.[108] Hydrogen bonding was the decisive factor that enabled this reaction. When ZnO was replaced with zinc nitrate, the hydrogen bonding between the metal salt
and 2-methylimidazole was lost and the reaction did not occur. Typically, the overall product yield from mechanical pressurization is lower than that from mechanical grinding, because the static force of mechanical pressurization only drives the MOF formation on the top layer of the M/MC surface, and cannot promote the production of more MOF layers. Nevertheless, solvent-free mechanical pressurization offers unique benefits such as straightforward control over the rate of MOF crystal growth at high pressures, and improved regulation of the thickness and shape of the MOFs.
## 2.2.2. Microsolvent-Assisted Solid-State Reaction
The low reactivity of solid-state \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) composite synthesis reactions limits their practical application. Certain solvents with high solubility or low boiling points, such as dimethylformamide, diethylformamide, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), and ethanol are commonly used as auxiliary solvents to potentially improve the fluidity of reactants, or to partially dissolve reactants to effectively ameliorate the synthesis-related limitation in the insoluble state. Low-boiling-point solvents have high rates of volatilization, leading to rapid drying of the reaction system and continuation of the synthesis.[109] For example, \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of methanol was mixed with 2-methylimidazole and zinc salt for grinding, which drove the generation of ZIF-8 on the surface in the solid state.[110]
To prevent the self-nucleation of MOFs in microsolvents and promote the controllable growth of MOFs on M/MCs, attempts have been made to pre-assemble the coordinated ligands (metals) and M/MC to form \(\mathrm{M/MC@}\) ligand systems, which then ensure the complete and uniform production of heterogeneous \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\). For example, Bellusci and coworkers grounded the 1,3,5-trichlorobenzoic acid ligand and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\) to modify the core, and then added the other reactants, metal ions, and trace amounts of the water/ethanol solvent to participate in the reaction, yielding FeMOF on the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) surface.[111] The pre-modification of ligands (metals) for the heterogeneous M/MC facilitates control of the MOF morphology and maintains the stability
of the M/MC during the grinding step under the protection of the premodified layers.
## 2.2.3. Summary
Solid-state reactions are environmentally friendly synthesis methods that can permit mass production of materials. However, owing to the slow and insufficient migration and contact of the \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) precursors in the solid state, the activity of the solid-state synthesis reactions is generally lower than that of liquid-phase synthesis, and the corresponding applications are generally limited to combining M/MCs and classic MOFs. Notably, microsolvent-assisted reactions exhibit higher reactivity than that of solvent-free solid-state reactions owing to the higher fluidity of their reactants. Solid-state reaction processes typically require the assistance of external mechanical forces. For instance, mechanical grinding is a dynamic process that alters the contact between reactants and shortens the reaction time, whereas mechanical pressurization is a static process that drives reactions through chemical interactions between reactants. However, the mechanical forces may dismantle the perfect crystalline structure and morphology of the MOFs, hinder the regulation of the uniform growth of MOFs around M/MCs, and diminish the control over the compactness of the interface; these are the major challenges that hinder the rapid development of solid-state synthesis methods (Table 1). Fortunately, multiple synthesis methods can be coordinated together to implement schemes such as self-sacrificial strategies, use of static pressures, and step-by-step mechanical synthesis, which can gradually overcome the aforementioned limitations and obtain a uniform crystalline phase.
## 2.3. Gas Phase Synthesis
ALD and CVD are the gas-phase deposition methods that are appropriate for the volatile precursors of M/MCs or MOFs. ALD is a self-limiting layer-by-layer sedimentation process that can accurately control the number, thickness, dispersion, and state of the deposition layers, whereas CVD installs layers that are independent of each other.
## 2.3.1. Atomic Layer Deposition
The self-limiting periodicity induced by continuous layer-by-layer deposition is the typical feature of ALD, which suggests that the number and size of particles can be precisely and uniformly controlled. By regulating the number of sedimentary layers, the subnanometer-scale thickness, and the deposition state, the M/MC in the MOF can be accurately modified, and high-density MOFs with controllable thickness and high coverage on the surface of presynthesized ALD-derived M/MC can be produced.[112]
\(M/M C@M O F s\) Based on Presynthesized ALD-M/MCs: \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) based on presynthesized ALD-M/MCs can be obtained using M/MCs presynthesized by ALD, and then growing heterogeneous MOFs around the M/MC in different modes according to the characteristics of the ALD-M/MC. This can be achieved in three ways: 1) direct nucleation of MOFs at the
interface with the ALD-M/MC; 2) one-step sacrificial nucleation, in which the ALD-M/MCs provide metal ions and diffuse to react with ligands; and 3) sacrificial-metal-induced nucleation of ALD-M/MCs, that is, the partially sacrificial metals of the ALD-derived M/MC cores bind to the metals of the MOFs and form bimetallic intermediates, which further react with ligands to generate crystalline MOFs. For instance, for one-step sacrificial nucleation, the ALD- \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) fibers in an \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) solution were partly sacrificed to provide \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) ions, which then combined with the diffused \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) ligand to form conformal MIL-96 on the \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) fiber surfaces.[113] Additionally, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) was found to not provide metal ions for UiO-66, UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), and UiO-67, and only offered nucleation sites and suitable roughness for the MOF growth; consequently, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) only promoted the direct nucleation of the MOFs on the outer layer (Figure 7a,b).[114] For the sacrificial-metal-induced nucleation of ALD-cores, the bimetallic intermediates connected by the metal ions of the MOFs and the sacrificial metals of the ALD-M/MCs are critical, which is the biggest difference in comparison with one-step sacrificial nucleation. M/MC@MOFs with different thicknesses can be obtained by changing the conversion ratio of the dual-salt intermediates. Parsons and co-workers immersed ALD-ZnO in a \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) solution and sacrificed it to form a \((\mathrm{Cu},\mathrm{Zn})\) hydroxyl nitrate HDS—\((\mathrm{Cu},\mathrm{Zn})(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{NO}_{3})\) (Figure 7c).[115] The \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)moieties of the \((\mathrm{Cu},\mathrm{Zn})(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{NO}_{3})\) intermediate could exchange with the ligand to promote the growth of HKUST-1. Assisted by the HDS, the amount of \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) also affected the conversion of the HDS, finally yielding \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\) with different HKUST-1 thicknesses. Evidently, the predeposited ALD-ZnO was key to the formation of the \((\mathrm{Cu},\mathrm{Zn})\) hydroxyl nitrate HDS, which determined the growth of HKUST-1 around the ALD-ZnO.
The compactness and uniformity of MOFs are closely related to the functional sites (such as \(-\mathrm{OH}\) and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)) and reactivity of the ALD-M/MCs. ALD-M/MCs with different functional groups exhibit varying adsorption-based interactive forces with the MOF precursors, affecting the diffusion of MOFs onto the ALD-cores.[116] Moreover, the different physical hygroscopicity and wettability of the ALD-M/MCs modify the nucleation sites of the MOFs on the interface with the ALD-M/MCs and provide different reactivity for the MOFs (Figure 7d– j).[117] Additionally, the ALD-M/MCs can flexibly and effectively achieve uniform distribution of the MOFs on the surface of the core and avoid the notable change in the interface between the MOFs and ALD-M/MCs, yielding \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOF}\) composites with high commonality, strong adhesion, and high controllability.
M/MC@MOFs Based on ALD-M/MC-Modified MOFs: An M/MC can be introduced into an MOF cavity by ALD, which overcomes the solubility limitation of the M/MC precursors.[118] Highly stable MOFs can be modified by ALD to achieve uniform functionalization of cavities. Generally, the deposition sites of the metal precursors are situated at the functional sites in MOFs, yielding metal-precursor@MOFs. Subsequently, the metal precursors are gradually reduced and then separate from the MOF cavity and the nanometals are aggregated inside the MOF. Hupp and co-workers comprehensively monitored the implantation of a Cu precursor into a Cu-MOF and the subsequent reduction to CuNPs by ALD. Upon entering the MOF, \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) combined with the oxygen atom of the Cu-MOF nodes, primarily forming \(\mathrm{CuO}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\). After the introduction of high-speed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gas, \(\mathrm{Cu}-\) Cu gradually appeared and detached from the MOF nodes until nanoparticles were formed and existed freely in the Cu-MOF cavity.[119]
The loading of an MOF onto an ALD-M/MC is linearly related to the number of ALD cycles of the ALD-M/MC. With increasing number of ALD cycles, the rates of diffusion and deposition of the ALD-M/MC in the MOF cavity decrease, because the ALD-M/MC gradually occupies the MOF cavity, affecting the subsequent deposition. Kim and co-workers sequentially incorporated a vapor precursor \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{Cp})_{2}\) of \(\mathrm{NiO}\) and an oxidant \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into the MIL-101 cavity to synthesize \(\mathrm{NiO}@\mathrm{MIL-101}\).[120] When \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{Cp})_{2}\) reached a saturated self-limiting state in each cycle, the deposition was terminated and the subsequent ALD cycle began. Moreover, the NiO precursor \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{Cp})_{2}\) could be selectively adsorbed onto the ligand sites. The deposited position and morphology of NiO in the MIL-101 cavity did not change during the self-limiting cyclic deposition, with only the number and thickness of NiO increasing gradually with increasing number of cycles. Notably, the pore volume of the MOF exhibited a non-step-by-step abrupt change. When the NiO deposition was initiated, NiO tended to inhabit all the mesopores of MIL-101 but not the micropores; subsequently, NiO tended to completely occupy the smaller pores after the complete deposition of NiO in the large mesopores.
## 2.3.2. Chemical Vapor Deposition
CVD is a type of solvent-free vapor growth technology in which gaseous reactants are introduced into the material of interest to form functional layers.[121] In 2005, Fischer et al. first applied CVD to modify MOFs with metals after their synthesis.[122] The vapor deposition temperature can vary from tens to hundreds of degrees Celsius according to the types of the MOF and deposited M/MC. CVD can be performed in vacuum or at atmospheric pressure, with vacuum conditions generally leading to a superior deposited morphology and M/MC distribution in MOF cavities.[123] The deposition of an M/MC into an MOF cavity occurs through four processes: diffusion, adsorption, surface reaction in the MOF cavity, and byproduct removal. The generation of \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MesMOF}\) clearly illustrates these four processes. First, the Ni precursor diffused and was then fixed in the MesMOF cavity at \(85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Subsequently, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) reacted with the Ni precursor in the MOF cavity at \(95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to form Ni and unnecessary cyclopentane molecules, which were then removed to obtain pure \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MesMOF}\).[124] The number of CVD layers can be regulated by repeating the deposition, because the deposits formed between each layer are independent of each other. Moreover, the thickness of the deposited M/MC can be controlled by changing the temperature, gas composition, concentration, and pressure.
The harsh reaction conditions and complex operating processes of CVD technology require the participation of volatile reactants and highly stable MOFs.[125,126] Nevertheless, combining the sacrificial template approach and CVD can simplify the deposition and prevent the destruction of the MOF skeleton under harsh conditions. For example, Zhao et al. employed \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) to provide \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) for preparing an MOF.[127] Briefly, 1,4-BDC was deposited onto the surface of the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) sacrificial core through CVD, forming \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL-101}\). At a fixed heating rate \((5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1})\), the deposition time of the 1,4-BDC ligand could change the number of 1,4-BDC molecules, further regulating the thickness of MIL-101 on the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) surface. Combined CVD/sacrificial template technology efficiently avoids the demand for highly stable MOFs, laying the foundation for the applications of CVD on M/MC@MOFs.
ble MOFs, laying the foundation for the applications of CVD on M/MC@MOFs.
## 2.3.3. Summary
ALD and CVD are deposition techniques that are based on gaseous substances; however, they have significant differences (Table 1). ALD is a self-limiting deposition process in which the extent of deposition can be controlled by the number of cycles, whereas CVD is a process in which the deposition of each layer is independent, and the thickness of the deposited M/MC can be varied by repeating the deposition. The main advantages of ALD and CVD include robust interfaces, controllable deposition thickness, and uniformity of the M/MC in the MOF. However, ALD and CVD have limited application potential owing to their relatively harsh reaction conditions, that is, high pressures and temperatures.
## 3. Applications of Heterogeneous M/MC@MOF Composites
## 3.1. Traditional Catalysis
The adsorption properties and screening capacity of MOFs, the protective and dispersive abilities of MOFs for M/MCs, the inherent catalytic sites of M/MCs and MOFs, and the morphology of MOFs can improve the performance of heterogeneous M/MC@MOF composites for traditional catalysis.[130] Additionally, the compositing of MOFs and M/MCs may enhance the active site exposure and M/MC–MOF contact create new electron-transfer pathways, enhancing the catalytic activity of the composites.
The basic roles of MOFs are to protect and fix the M/MCs through their restrictive effect, which promotes the catalytic activity and cycling properties of M/MC@MOFs. For instance, in \(\mathrm{CuPd}@\mathrm{MIL-101}\) and \(\mathrm{PtCu}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\), the external MOFs protected the internal \(\mathrm{CuPd}\) and \(\mathrm{PtCu}\) components, respectively. Consequently, the crystal phase and catalytic activity were preserved after the \(\mathrm{CuPd}@\mathrm{MIL-101}\)-catalyzed coupling of phenylacetylene and the \(\mathrm{PtCu}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\)-catalyzed hydrogenation of 1-hexene, cis-octene, and styrene.[131,132] The restrictive ability of MOFs for M/MCs prevents their uncontrollable migration and aggregation, maintaining the activity. For example, \(\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{UiO-bpy}\) exhibited high catalytic activity for the direct conversion of methane to methanol.[133] When \(\mathrm{U}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)-bpy was destroyed, the small \(\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{x}\) clusters partially converted to \(\mathrm{CuO}\) nanomaterials because of the thermal motion in the reaction, leading to a loss
in catalytic activity for methane oxidation. However, the MOF stabilized \(\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{x}\) and prevented its transformation into inactivated \(\mathrm{CuO}\). Moreover, UiO-bpy promoted the dispersion of the small \(\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{x}\) particles to further improve the catalytic activity. Similarly, the restriction and protection of Pd by ZIF-8 improved the catalytic reactivity of Pd@ZIF-8 for the hydrogenation of 1-hexene and styrene.[106]
The M/MC dispersion of MOFs can regulate the exposure of catalytically active sites on the M/MCs.[134] For example, the catalytic activity of heterogeneous Pd@NMOF-Ni has been related to the dispersion of the Pd catalytic center, which increased the number of active sites exposed to the catalytic system.[135] Wen
and co-workers prepared Ni@NMOF-0.4 (the number represents the concentration of the reductant-added NMOF) and found that the Ni NPs in Ni@NMOF-0.4 were only 1.2 nm in size on average (Figure 8a–c).[72] The uniform dispersion of Ni enabled the composite to exhibit considerably higher catalytic ability in the catalytic reduction of 4-nitrophenol than that of pure NMOF and Ni@NMOF-0.2 (0.6) (Figure 8d,e). Increasing the dispersion of CuNi led to CuNi@MIL-101 exhibiting increased contact between the catalytically active sites for aminoborane dehydrogenation and nitroaromatic hydrogenation, significantly improving the catalytic activity of the cascade reactions.[136] Considering the dispersion of M/MCs by MOFs, the regulation of MOFs for
enabling contact between the M/MCs and catalytic substrates also affects the catalytic behavior of the composites. Kitagawa and co-workers synthesized Pd@UiO-66 with markedly enhanced oxidation activity for CO. The composite modified the active Pd–CO contact by altering the adsorption properties of the independent Pd for the CO reactant.[137] The adsorption of hollow Pd sites onto CO inhibited the catalytic oxidation for CO; however, UiO-66 prevented the activity inhibition of the hollow Pd sites for CO and induced CO to enter the top and bridge sites with higher Pd activity, intensifying the CO–O interactions on the Pd surface and promoting the CO oxidation.
The MOFs that surround M/MCs as the first barrier can also improve the catalytic selectivity of M/MC@MOF composites owing to the inherent screening function of their pores; alternatively, they can enhance the catalytic conversion rate owing to the different adsorption or migration rates of the reactants in specific pores. For example, the high yield of \(\mathrm{CuBr}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101 through the cyclosylation reaction between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and an alkynyl alcohol or alkynyl amine was mainly due to the selective adsorption and transport of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) by the MOF, which increased the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentration on the surface of the CuBr catalytic center and significantly increased the catalytic activity.[138] Huo and co-workers and Li and co-workers studied the effect of pore size on the catalytic activity of the composites. In the static hydrogenation conversion of \(n\) -pentene, \(n\) -hexene, \(n\) -heptene, and cis-cyclooctene, the overall catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) improved gradually with increasing pore size owing to the higher diffusion speed and enlarged migration space of the reactant in the larger pores.[139] Similarly,
the MOF of \(\mathrm{PtNi}@\mathrm{Ni-MOF-74}\) selectively transported the smallest molecule (styrene) among four mixed olefin substrates to the PtNi surface by leveraging its size-screening function, which boosted the selective hydrogenation of styrene and inhibited the transformation of the coupling product of aniline and benzaldehyde to imine (Figure 8f,g).[140] Moreover, the size screening and confinement effects of UiO-67 regulated the catalytic selectivity of \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}67\) for phenylacetylene hydrogenation.[141] Ag diluted the surface of Pd to generate isolated, discontinuously exposed Pd sites, which suppressed the excessive hydrogenation of phenylacetylene; consequently, the selectivity for styrene increased gradually with decreasing number of exposed sites.
of methyl cyclopentane to acyclic isomers, olefins, cyclohexane, and benzene, because of the different chemical environments of the MOFs.[144] Owing to the presence of the strongly acidic \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) sites, \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) exhibited the highest selectivity for cyclohexane and benzene, whereas \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-NH}_{3}^{+}\)with the weakly acid group exhibited a two times lower catalytic activity and halved lower selectivity for the cyclization products. These results clearly highlighted the effects of the acidic and basic functionalities of the MOFs on the activity and selectivity of the
composites for the catalytic reaction. Moreover, the acidic sites of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-NH}_{3}^{+}\)were neutralized to form neutralized \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-NH}_{2}\) catalysts, which exhibited activities similar to that of the initial \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66}\); \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na-NH}_{2}\) also showed similar results. Additionally, the partially neutralized \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na-NH}_{3}^{+}\)catalyst exhibited activity similar to that of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{nUiO-66-NH}_{3}^{+}\), further verifying the influence of the chemical environment on the catalytic behavior.
Most of the aforementioned factors that influence the traditional catalytic function of M/MC@MOF composites are not independent; moreover, they are related to the charge-transfer abilities of the MOFs, M/MCs, and reaction substrates. In particular, improvements in charge transfer are critical to enhancing the catalytic activity; consequently, the charge-transfer capacity is a crucial parameter in the design of most M/MC@MOF catalysts. For example, Kitagawa and co-workers soaked Zr-UiO-66-X
specimens separately in a Cu precursor solution—Cu(acac)2— to prepare Cu@MOF-X composite catalysts, which catalyzed the CO2 hydrogenation to methanol.[145] The reduction in the MOF binding energy significantly promoted the charge transfer from Cu nanoparticles to the MOF interface. The methanol yields from Cu@Zr-UiO-66 and Cu@Zr-UiO-66-COOH were approximately 70 and 210 times higher than that of Cu/γ-Al2O3. In traditional catalysis systems, the internal synergy of MOFs as well
as the MOF-MM/C interactions regulate the charge-transfer attributes of the MOFs, M/MCs, and reactants. For instance, Lin and co-workers assembled \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}@\mathrm{UiO}\) -bpy and investigated the cooperative synergy between the MOF and \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}\) .[146] The Zr cluster, nano \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{\mathrm{i}}\) , and \(Z\mathrm{nO}_{x}\) of \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}@\mathrm{UiO}\) -bpy initially adsorbed \({\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) , and the \({\mathrm{Cu}}\) sites subsequently activated and dissociated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to form \(_\mathrm{H}\) atoms. \(Z\mathrm{nO}_{x}\) and the \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\) secondary building units then activated \({\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\) , which was acted upon by the dissociated \(_\mathrm{H}\) to produce methanol with high yield and high selectivity. The synergistic interactions between the catalytic substrates, the external MOFs, and the internal M/MCs are key to improving the catalytic activity. Generally, synergistic forces such as \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions, hydrogen bonding, and van der Waals forces are closely related to the electron-transfer pathways and efficiency of nanomaterials and MOFs. Huang and co-workers changed the functional groups of the ligands in UiO-66 to investigate \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{X}\) catalysts \(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{H}\) , \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) , OMe) for the aerobic reaction between benzaldehyde and ethylene glycol.[147] The electron-transfer pathway established between the \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) group and Pd nanoclusters in \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) enabled \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) to provide electrons to Pd, which weakened the oxidative ability of the composites, finally yielding acetals as nonoxidizing products with high selectivity. Moreover, the \(-\mathrm{H}\) and –OMe groups led to negligible MOF–PdNC coordination; consequently, \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\) and \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{OMe}\) catalyzed the formation of esters as oxidation products.
The thickness, morphology, and shape of the MOFs also guide the catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) . For instance, Maspoch and co-workers synthesized \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8\) and \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) with different MOF thicknesses; they denoted \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8\) with MOF thicknesses of \(66\pm42\) , \(56\pm64\) , and \(236\pm64\;\mathrm{nm}\) as Pt-Z1, Pt-Z-2, and Pt-Z-3, respectively, and termed \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8\) with MOF thicknesses of \(55\pm24\) , \(148\,\pm\,50\) , and \(585\,\pm\,135\mathrm{nm}\) as Pd-Z-1, Pd-Z-2, and Pd-Z-3, respectively.[148] Pt-Z-1 and Pd-Z-1 exhibited the highest reaction rates among the composites for reducing 4-nitrophenol and eosin Y, because the thinner MOFs facilitated the diffusion of the reactants from the MOF to the nanosized Pt or Pd. In particular, for the \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8\) -induced catalysis of eosin Y, the conversion rate gradually increased with decreasing MOF thickness, because eosin Y was larger than 4- nitrophenol and could not readily traverse the MOF. However, this result does not imply that the catalytic effect always intensified with decreasing shell thickness. Additionally, the effect of the shell thickness on catalytic activity and selectivity was not necessarily consistent. Kuang and co-workers used Rh– Ni nanoflowers to construct RhNi@MOF-74(Ni) with different shell thicknesses, with the Ni content mainly determining the
thickness of the \(\mathrm{RhNi@MOF(Ni)}\) shell (Figure 10a–d).[149] The composite exhibited high catalytic activity and selectivity for the hydrogenation of diphenylacetylene to cis-stilbene (Figure 10e). With increasing MOF layer thickness, the conversion of the reactant decreased, but the selectivity toward the cis products increased. Adjusting shape, interface, composition, and thickness of the M/MCs and MOFs can regulate the catalytic reactivity of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) . For example, Chen and co-workers synthesized \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL-}53(\mathrm{Al})\) by depositing a self-sacrificial \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) layer onto the \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{Pd}\) surface (Figure 10f).[150] Compared to the original \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{Pd}\) , the composite catalyst contained micropores in its interlayers owing to the introduction of the MOF, which increased the catalytic activity and selectivity for the furfural hydrogenation to furfural alcohol (85.3%) and the selectivity for the cuminaldehyde hydrogenation to culmin alcohol (100%). Moreover, the structure of the MOF limited the number of Pd reaction sites on the benzene ring, preventing the hydrogenation of the benzene ring.
These reports open more ideas for the deep development of heterogeneous \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) catalysts. If \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) cannot meet the performance, sandwich structures \(\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) can be as a good choice. Tang and co-workers synthesized sandwich-structured UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67, which exhibited a 100% conversion rate and 93.3% selectivity for styrene in the hydrogenation of phenylacetylene.[151] Both the hydrophobic MOF core and MOF shell aided in the abundant accumulation of acetylene on the surface of active Pd, whereas UiO-67 modified the electronic state of Pd to ensure that it was enriched with electrons for increasing the catalytic activity. Specifically, the interfacial electron transfer between UiO-67 and Pd was key to achieving high selectivity. The catalytic performance of sandwich-structured composite catalysts can be regulated by the thickness of the MOF shells. For example, ZIF-8@Au25@ZIF-67 composites with different ZIF-67 thicknesses were assembled for the \({\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\) -based carboxylation of terminal alkynes.[152] The catalytic activity increased and then decreased with increasing ZIF-67 shell thickness, because the shell not only affected the stability of the \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) catalytic center and the transfer of reactants, but also altered the \({\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\) adsorption. Therefore, excessively thick shells inhibited the catalytic activity, and ZIF-8@Au25@ZIF-6712 achieved a 99% yield. Notably, ZIF-8@Au25@ZIF-6712 exhibited higher activity than that of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8\) and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}/\mathrm{ZIF-}8\) , which also verified the effectiveness of the dual-interface effect. In a word, sandwich-structured \(\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) provide a new approach for designing MOF composite catalysts.
Overall, the diverse features of M/MCs enable them to exhibit different application-based features for catalytic reactions. The protection, dispersion, fixation, and restriction of M/MCs by MOFs are particularly related to the MOF characteristics, which directly affect the exposure of active sites on the M/MCs. Moreover, the MOF characteristics also regulate the migration and transfer of specific reaction substrates, influencing the catalytic activity and selectivity. Notably, the MOF-M/MC synergy is a key factor that ensures that the \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) composites catalytically outperform the M/MCs, MOFs, and physical mixtures of M/MCs and MOFs. This synergy not only induces more efficient contact and reactions between the substrates with M/MCs and MOFs, but also improves the charge transfer efficiency.
Therefore, M/MC@MOFs exhibit enhanced catalytic activity and cycling stability, which is also related to the robust interface between the heterogeneous MOFs and M/MCs; these are the unique advantages of heterogeneous M/MC@MOF composites.
## 3.2. Photocatalysis
Through photocatalytic reactions, certain materials can convert light energy into chemical energy and then transform reactants into desired products in a short period.[153] M/MC@MOFs
exhibit pronounced advantages in terms of photocatalytic performance, with the MOF-M/MC interactions facilitating energy/charge transfer in photocatalytic reactions. For instance, Horcajada and co-workers applied photoinduced transformation to assemble \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Figure 11a).[154] The strong interactions between \(\mathrm{Ag}\) and the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group of the MOF enhanced the optical energy transmission and visible-light absorption of the composite. Compared to MIL- \(125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{MIL}\) - \(125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) exhibited significantly improved photodegradation abilities for methylene blue, sulfamethazine, and \(p\) -nitroaniline. The tight connections and dense interfaces of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\)
are particularly beneficial because they permit efficient synergy between the composite parts. Jiang and co-workers synthesized \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}@{\mathrm{HKUST}}{\mathrm{-}}1\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{HKUST}{-}1\), which had a robust interface and randomly grown HKUST-1 that partially covered the outer \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\) layer, respectively.[46] \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{HKUST}{-}1\) exhibited a notably inferior catalytic activity compared to that of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{HKUST}{-}1\), because its loose interface led to a weak synergistic effect and hindered the catalytic activity. In contrast, owing to the coordination between the Lewis acidic sites on HKUST-1 and the reductive sites on \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\), \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{HKUST}{-}1\) catalyzed the entire cascade reaction of nitrobenzene, \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}\), and benzaldehyde under light irradiation, achieving \(100\%\) selectivity for the coupling product of benzaldehyde and aniline (Figure 11b–e).
Various noble metals have been used in heterogeneous photocatalysts. In particular, noble metals can act as electron collectors in collaboration with MOFs, resulting in higher electron–hole separation efficiencies. For instance, Su and co-workers synthesized \(0.9\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{Pd}\) -PCN-222(Hf) with uniform distribution and fixed orientation (Figure 12a).[155] Pt promoted the electron–hole separation and cooperated with the photosensitive ligand Pd-porphyrin, eventually producing hydrogen
Various noble metals have been used in heterogeneous photocatalysts. In particular, noble metals can act as electron collectors in collaboration with MOFs, resulting in higher electron–hole separation efficiencies. For instance, Su and co-workers synthesized \(0.9\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{Pd}\) -PCN-222(Hf) with uniform distribution and fixed orientation (Figure 12a).[155] Pt promoted the electron–hole separation and cooperated with the photosensitive ligand Pd-porphyrin, eventually producing hydrogen (22674 \(\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\); Figure 12b). Dispersed noble metals can considerably promote the transfer of photogenerated electrons from electron–hole pairs,[156] whereas MOFs can protect these noble-metals in high-radiation environments, which is the typical function of MOFs.[157] However, noble metal photocatalysts have restricted applicability owing to their high price and limited resources. Therefore, non-noble-metal catalysts that can increase the visible-light absorption or electron–hole separation efficiency—such as CdS, \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\), and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{P}_{y}\)—have been developed to improve the photocatalytic performance of MOFs. For instance, Hou and co-workers assembled an inexpensive CdS@MOF-808 composite.[77] CdS altered the bandgap width and improved the carrier-separation efficiency of the MOF (Figure 12c,d). Owing to the cooperation between the high specific surface area of MOF-808 and the catalytic sites of CdS, CdS@MOF-808 catalyzed the coupling of aliphatic amines to form monoamines under visible light. The simple, sustainable nano- \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\) cocatalyst was introduced into \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti), which partially reduced \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\) to \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) with lower conduction-band-related work function.[158] \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\) broadened the visible-light absorption range of the MOF and inhibited the electron–hole
recombination at the junction with \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) sites, promoting charge-carrier generation and separation. Additionally, the hydrogen production rate of the \(1\,\mathrm{wt}\%\,\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) composite catalyst was significantly higher than that of \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL125(Ti) (11055.5, 624.2, and \(394.3\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) , respectively).
Essentially, effective photoelectron-transfer pathways and inhibited electron–hole recombination are key to enhancing the photocatalytic properties of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) . For example, in \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag@ZIF-8}\) , robust contact enhanced the photogenerated-electron-transfer characteristics of the M/MCs and MOFs, and inhibited the electron–hole recombination. Consequently, the photocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL-NH}_{2}\) for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER; \(4383.1\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and that of \(\mathrm{Ag@ZIF-8}\) for the degradation of rhodamine B \(93\%\) conversion) and methyl orange (MO; \(100\%\) conversion) were excellent.[159,160] Similarly, the \(\mathrm{Pt@PCN-777}\) catalyst exhibited decent photocatalytic activity for benzylamine oxidation and the HER.[161] The highest occupied and lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals of PCN-777 were spatially separated; therefore, the photogenerated electrons and holes of \(\mathrm{Pt@PCN-777}\) were effectively uncoupled. The independent holes further oxidized aniline, and the electrons concurrently promoted the decomposition and reduction of water to generate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) . The yields of aniline as an oxidation and reduction product upon the catalysis of \(\mathrm{Pt@PCN-777}\) were 486 and \(332\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) , respectively, with the optimal HER yield being \(586~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) . Furthermore, the light absorption capabilities of M/MCs and MOFs are also crucial for improving the photocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) . For example, Xue and co-workers assembled \(\mathrm{Ni}_{x}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125 (x: relative Ni loading in the MOF).[162] The light absorption capacity of the composite improved with increasing Ni loading. However, excess Ni affected the optical absorption bandwidth and provided
recombination sites for the photogenerated carriers, thereby suppressing the photocatalytic effect. Therefore, \(\mathrm{Ni}_{\mathrm{XeO}_{2}}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL125 achieved an optimal HER efficiency \((699\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) . Hou and co-workers manufactured heterogenous \(\mathrm{BiOI@NU-1000}\) exhibited an HER rate of up to \(610\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) due to wider light-absorption-band and higher photoinduced-charge-carrier separation efficiency of the composite than that of NU-1000.[163] In fact, both the electron-hole separation efficiency and the construction of photoelectron-transfer pathways in \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) are highly correlated with the MOF-M/MC synergy. For example, the activity of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{BTC})\) for the photocatalytic MB degradation was higher than that of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{BTC})\) because of the MOF-M/MC synergy.[164] In this system, MOF acted as an antenna and received visible light; holes and photogenerated electrons were produced; and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) subsequently received the electrons. This electron-transmission pathway inhibited the electron–hole recombination, and the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) acted upon by the photoexcited electrons and \(\mathrm{h^{+}}\) participated in the MB degradation.
The inherent adsorption capacities of MOFs for reactants can also improve the photocatalytic reaction efficiency. For example, the adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) for tetracycline (TC) was higher than that of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) , because the inherent adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) , the MOF- \(\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) synergy, and the intermolecular electrostatic attraction collectively promoted the adsorption of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) for TC, improving the subsequent photodegradation of TC to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) .[165] Therefore, the photocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) for TC degradation was notably superior to that of the pure MOF and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) . Similarly, the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group in \(\mathrm{CuNCs@UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) exhibited higher affinity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , which was key to improving the efficiency of photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to CO and HCOOH.[166]
As rigid shells, MOFs can partly enhance the stability of M/MCs to prevent photocorrosion. For instance, Han and co-workers introduced CdS and \(C0^{2+}\) into UiO-bpy, enhancing the photogenerated electron transfer and electron–hole separation.[167] Moreover, UiO-bpy protected CdS from corrosion and prevented the loss of \(C0^{2+}\) in the reaction, enhancing the cycling activity. The selectivity and yield of the ternary \(\mathrm{CdS@UiO-bpy/Co}\) composite for the photocatalytic reduction of \(C O_{2}\) to CO ( \(85\%\) and \(235\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\) \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\,\mathsf{h}^{-1})\) were 10.6 and 10.2 times those of CdS, respectively.
In addition, plasmonic metals are unique materials that can produce localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) and surface plasmon polariton (SPP) under light irradiation, and consequently affect the photocatalytic activity of materials.[168] When LSPR-exhibiting metal characteristics match with the absorption characteristics of photocatalytic active center, the LSPR-exhibiting metals can control the spatial localization of MOF skeletons through the generated environmental electric field, enhancing the photocatalytic activity of the MOF composites.[169] Guiding the electrons of the photocatalytically active center to toward the enhanced electric field can also strengthen the photocatalytic activity through the plasma resonance electric field.
The influences of plasmonic metals on the photocatalytic activity of MOFs are related to the surface electric field of LSPR-exhibiting metals generated under light irradiation, and related to the thickness of MOFs in which the surface electric field of plasmonic attenuates with increasing surface distance. Therefore, the MOF layer thickness is a critical factor that affects the plasmonic \(\mathrm{M@MOF}\) performance. For example, Yaghi and coworkers introduced plasmonic Ag nanoparticles into \(\mathrm{Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}\) , and investigated the effect of the near-surface electric field on the photocatalytic reduction of \(C O_{2}\) by MOFs with different thicknesses (Figure 13a,b).[170] Under light irradiation, the photoac
tive center of the \(\mathrm{Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}\) was located in the intensified near-surface electric fields of the Ag core at the SPR frequency, leading to a fivefold increase in the catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Ag@Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}.\) - \(33\mathrm{nm}\) than that of \(\mathrm{Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}\) . Furthermore, to explore the optimal electric field effect of the plasmons on the MOF, the thickness of the MOF was reduced to ensure that the photocatalytic active center was maximally affected by the electric field. When the thickness of \(\mathrm{Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}\) was \(16\;\mathrm{nm}\) , the photocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Ag@Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}16\mathrm{nm}\) for the reduction of \(C O_{2}\) increased again compared to \(\mathrm{Ag@Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}33\;\mathrm{nm}\) , which was seven times more than \(\mathrm{Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}\) (Figure 13c,d). Notably, the enhanced photocatalytic activity was not observed when the plasmonic \(\mathrm{Ag}\) was replaced with similarly sized \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{\mathrm{i}}\) , emphasizing the significant contribution of the correspondence between the plasmonic \(\mathrm{Ag}\) and the absorption spectrum of the MOF. When the electrons from the surface collective resonance were coupled with each other, an elementary excitation with a strong surface magnetic field was produced. Excitons moved on the metal surface, with the maximum effect occurring when the distance traversed by the excitons was consistent with the size of the material. Under light irradiation, the excitons guided or participated in the electron transfer through electron coupling and interfacial motion after absorbing photons, promoting the catalytic activity of the \(\mathrm{Ag@Re}_{3}\mathrm{MOF}\) composite photocatalyst. The photocatalytic behavior of plasmonic metals could also be enhanced by the photothermal effect, in which plasmonic metals convert light into heat energy through electron–phonon coupling and promote hot electron generation, increasing the reactivity of \(\mathrm{M@MOF}\) composites for reactions. For example, Kuang and co-workers compared the photothermal behavior of \(\mathrm{Au@UIO-66-NH}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{Au@Pd@UIO-66-NH}_{2}{\cdot}0.5\) , and \(\mathrm{Au@Pd@UiO-66-NH}_{2}{\cdot}2\) under light irradiation (0.5, 2: thickness of the Pd layer enveloping Au), and observed temperature
changes of 0.24, 0.36, and \(0.58~\mathrm{mK}\), respectively, indicating differences between the plasmonic Au and Pd in terms of relative heating.[171] In particular, the Pd on the surface of Au exhibited a long-duration window of hot electron transmission and effectively delayed the energy dissipation of Au, whereas the outermost MOF protected and promoted the hot electron generation of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Pd}\). However, excessively thick Pd affected the light-absorbing ability of Au; therefore, \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}-0.5\) exhibited optimal photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction \((3737\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{metal}}^{-1}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) owing to the photothermal effect. Jiang and co-workers synthesized the composite catalyst \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MIL}-125/\mathrm{Au}\) by combining SPP metal and MOF.[172] The plasmon band of the Au nanoparticles coincided well with the absorption wavelength of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MIL}-125/\mathrm{Au}\); consequently, the hot electrons from Au were injected into the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital of MIL-125 to produce \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\), leading to an enriched-electron-storing Pt center that promoted \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation. The hydrogen production rate and number of cycles were high \((1743.0\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) and 170, respectively). Therefore, the contribution of plasmonic metals in MOF-based photocatalytic systems is particularly noteworthy; however, plasmonic \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{MOFs}\) have been rarely reported owing to the scarcity of plasmonic metals.
The aforementioned discussion suggests that existing photocatalytic composites can be mainly categorized into conventional \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\) and plasmonic \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{MOFs}\). For conventional \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\), the most common approaches for improving the photocatalytic property involve strategies that enhance the MOF-M/MCs cooperation, such as expanding the electron-transport path, improving the electron–hole separation efficiency, increasing the electron–proton coupling efficiency, employing adsorbing reaction substrates, and selecting target products. For conventional \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\), the most common approaches for improving the photocatalytic property involve strategies that enhance the MOF-M/MCs cooperation, such as expanding the electron-transport path, improving the electron–hole separation efficiency, increasing the electron–proton coupling efficiency, employing adsorbing reaction substrates, and selecting target products.
## 3.3. Electrocatalysis
\(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\) also have decent development prospects in electrocatalysis. In particular, \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\) have been widely studied in the field of electrocatalytic oxygen evolution reaction (OER), oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), and HER. Pt-based materials with effective and stable electrocatalytic properties are usually introduced into MOFs to enhance their electrocatalytic activity. For instance, Li and co-workers engaged a hydrogen-evolving Pt catalyst into \(\mathrm{Zn-MOF}\) to assemble an HER electrocatalyst, which had an overpotential of only \(23\,\mathrm{mV}\) at a current density of \(10\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\).[173] The path through which electrons were transferred from the MOF to nano-Pt was critical to enhancing the catalytic activity of the composite. Attempts have been made since then to develop superior, cost-effective electrocatalysts. Ag nanorods were applied to assemble \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZIF}-67\), which can perform electrocatalytic OER and ORR.[174] Ag nanorods were used to assemble \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZIF}-67\), which showed activity for the electrocatalytic OER and ORR. \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZIF}-67\) had a more apparent metal–
electronic structure than that of pure ZIF-67 and Ag; therefore, \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZIF}-67\) exhibited a higher negative starting potential and current density. In the ORR, the ZIF-67 pores induced Ag to fully react with \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), improving the electrocatalytic ORR activity of \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZIF}-67\).
Single-electron metals such as Fe and Co are conducive to electron transport; therefore, MOFs with single-electron metals are preferred, or MOFs are introduced to M/MCs with single electrons to achieve high electron-transport efficiency. For instance, in the tetrametallic electrocatalyst \(\mathrm{NF}@\mathrm{FeCoMnNi-MOF-74}\), the valence transition and synergistic effect of redox electrons on \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}/\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}/\mathrm{Mn}^{4+}\) primarily directed the efficient charge transfer.[2] Moreover, the composite provided more active sites for the reaction with the electrolyte to promote charge transfer during the catalysis. The cell voltage was only \(1.62\,\mathrm{V}\) when the composite was used as an anode and cathode for real water splitting at a current density of \(10\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) in a \(1\,\mathrm{M}\) electrolyte. To widen the applicability of single-electron metals, which are a popular choice for electrocatalytic materials, mixed M/MCs with single electrons can be synthesized and introduced into MOFs. Chen and coworkers introduced polymetallic FeNi-layered double hydroxide (LDH) into a polymetallic MOF to produce an ultrathin (1.34 nm sized) FeNi-LDH@FeNi-MOF composite (Figure 14a).[175] The overpotential of the composite for the OER was only 272 \(\mathrm{mV}@\mathrm{100\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}}\), which was attributed to the contributions of FeNi. Moreover, the cycling stability and durability of the composite were excellent. In fact, the MOF protected the individual M/MCs from the high current density and the \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the electrolyte system and enhanced the stability and durability of the composites.[134]
The above discussion focuses on the OER-, HER-, and ORR-related applications of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOF}\) electrocatalysis. Compared with these, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})\) is more complicated because it involves multiple reactions with different products and overlapping intermediates.[176] Moreover, the multiple reactions have similar electrochemical potentials, leading to low selectivity for desired products; this problem should be carefully considered while designing electrocatalytic materials.[177] The HER also occurs easily during the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), which also affects the electrocatalytic selectivity of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\mathrm{MOFs}\). The study found that the desired selectivity of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) with inhibited HER can be achieved through regulating the interaction between the composite and specific reaction intermediate, the efficiency of proton transport or the structure of \(\mathrm{M/MC}\).[178] The Faradaic efficiency (FE) of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production by the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) electrocatalyst \(\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\) increased from \(10\%\) to \(45\%\), and that of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was reduced to below \(7\%\) owing to the adjustment of the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) clusters from symmetric to asymmetric motifs.[179] Cao et al. synthesized \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CuHHTP}\) as a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) electrocatalyst, in which several uncoordinated \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups were distributed around the active \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) owing to the partial rupture of coordination bonds.[180] \(-\mathrm{OH}\) stabilized the intermediates of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) product \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) (such as \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{OCH}_{3}\)) by forming hydrogen bonds, which was key to achieving the high selectivity. Moreover, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{CuHHTP}\) exhibited a high FE (73%) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-to-\(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) conversion and a partial current density of \(10.8\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). Similarly, \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) stabilized the \(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\)intermediate through the MOF–intermediate interactions, and modulated the
proton transfer to the actives site to reduce the efficiency of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation, increasing the selectivity of the electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) generation for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\).[181] The selectivity of the electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) for CO reached \(90\%\) at a potential of \(-0.8\mathrm{~V~}\) versus reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE).
The activity and selectivity of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) composites for the electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) are also related to the MOF-M/MC synergistic effects. For example, AgNCs introduced into Al-PMOF showed better morphological stability and size adjustability than pure AgNC.[182] The MOF not only enhanced the exposure of the AgNC sites, but also transported \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) during the electrocatalysis. Moreover, the robust interface between the AgNCs and AlPMOF guaranteed electron transport from the MOF to the AgNCs, thereby promoting electron transfer from the AgNCs to the reactant \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Essentially, a complete cocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) was achieved. Therefore, the CO selectivity of \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{Al-PMOF}\) at \(-1.1\,\mathrm{V}\) \((55.8\%)\) was 2.3 times higher than that of the AgNCs. The MOF\(\mathrm{M/MC}\) synergistic effects, such as the transport of reactants by the MOFs, the exposure of sites, and the charge-transport path provided by the interface, are all the important factor for enhancing electrocatalytic activity and selectivity. As for the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cu}-\) MOF electrocatalyst, the high surface area of the MOF led to strong \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption, and the MOF had more active sites owing to the growth on the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) surface.[183] Additionally, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) increased the charge-transfer efficiency of the MOF. The bidirectional collaboration between \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and Cu-MOF led to an excellent \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-to- \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) conversion efficiency. The FE of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cu}-\) MOF with respect to \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity was as high as \(63.2\%\) \((-1.71\,\mathrm{V})\). The composite electrocatalyst assembled using 1.4–3.3
nm sized \(\mathrm{CuO}\) and 2D Cu-MOF nanosheets not only provided numerous active sites for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), but also possessed a robust interface that made the active sites more accessible; this enhanced the adsorption of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reactant by the composite and facilitated functional activation of the MOF and \(\mathrm{CuO}\) and their synergy.[184] At an RHE potential of \(-1.1\,\mathrm{V}\), the faradic efficiency for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production by this composite \((50\%)\) was higher than that of \(\mathrm{CuO}\) \((25.5\%)\) and \(\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) \((37.6\%)\).
The \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\)-producing \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) reduction reaction (NRR) is also being targeted for investigation with \(\mathrm{M/M@MOF}\) electrocatalysts. However, \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) have not been extensively developed for the electrocatalytic NRR, because the inertness \((\mathrm{N} \equiv \mathrm{N}\) bond strength \(=941\,\mathrm{KJ\,mol^{-1}})\) and solubility of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) in water hinder the catalytic activity, and the extremely narrow potential windows of the HER and NRR affect the selectivity of the NRR.[185] Consequently, effective strategies for enhancing the NRR performance of \(\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{MC-MOFs}\) are aimed at eliminating the competition between the HER and NRR.[186] For example, in the \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) composite assembled using nanoporous gold and an MOF, the internal Au and external MOF acted as the catalytically active center and transport channel, respectively. In particular, the hydrophobic and porous MOF weakened the diffusion of the reactant and inhibited the competitive HER, which led to the \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) NRR electrocatalyst exhibiting a high FE \((44\%)\) at \(-0.6\,\mathrm{V}\) and excellent \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) selectivity \((98\%)\).[187] By exploiting the hydrophobicity and molecular concentration effects of the external MOF, Liu et al. also improved the electrocatalytic NRR activity and selectivity.[188] ZIF-71 blocked the entry of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and promoted the aggregation of the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) reactant on the surface of
the Ag-Au catalytic center, which led to Ag-Au@ZIF-71 exhibiting a FE of \(18\%\) and a selectivity of \(90\%\) for the electrocatalytic NRR. The external MOF undeniably plays an important role in inhibiting the HER and promoting the NRR by utilizing its screening and transfer effects. Additionally, the MOF imparts high stability to the M/MC catalytic center. Therefore, the advantages of MOFs can be exploited to develop new NRR catalytic materials.
Overall, conductivity and stability are generally the basic requirements of electrocatalysts. The integration of M/MCs and MOFs solves their issues including poor electrical conductivity and corrosion resistance. The screening and transfer ability of MOFs for reactants, intermediates, and products also help increase the electrocatalytic efficiency. Moreover, the durable MOF-M/MC interface provides an ordered electron-transport path and bestows electrical corrosion resistance to the composite catalysts. Early research on \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) electrocatalysts was targeted toward the HER, ORR, and OER owing to the relative simplicity of these reactions, the lack of excessive side reactions, and fewer electron-transfer quantities required to complete these reactions. In contrast, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) involves multiple electron/proton-transfer processes and must overcome a high kinetic barrier; additionally, the competitive HER and multiple reactions occurring during this process must be addressed. Developing \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) with high activity is more difficult for the NRR, mainly because of the inertness of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and the competitive HER. Therefore, the selectivity of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) composites during the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and NRR is a key concern in research. Nevertheless, the unique screening characteristics of MOFs can shield \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and promote the accumulation of reactants in the M/MC catalytic center, thus achieving high selectivity; this provides the design direction of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and NRR.
## 3.4. Sensing
Sensing attributes are closely related to characteristics such as roughness, porosity, size, and crystal morphology. The combination of MOFs and M/MCs can selectively screen the desired materials and improve their surface characteristics, thus promoting the sensitivity and selectivity. The recognition ability of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) composite sensors can be examined by measuring the changes in conductivity, fluorescence, heat, mass, or capacitance.
\(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\)-composite-based electrochemical sensors exhibit high sensitivity and selectivity owing to the easily detectable changes in their electrochemical properties (including current, conductivity, resistance, and potential). The corresponding detection limit can be as low as \(10^{-12}\) mol \(\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), such as that of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{MOF}(\mathrm{II})\) for diethylstilbestrol \((1.0 \times 10^{-12}-1.0 \times 10^{-4}\) mol \(\mathrm{L}^{-1})\), and that of \(\mathrm{AuNCs@NMOFs}\) for hypochlorite \((8 \times 10^{-8}-1.0 \times 10^{-3}\) mol \(\mathrm{L}^{-1})\).[189,190] The electrical sensing characteristics of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) may depend on the different electrochemical properties of the composites when in contact with the detected substances. The identified materials may influence the charge distribution of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\), enabling the composites to exhibit different electrical performances. For example, Kim and co-workers synthesized an electrochemical \(\mathrm{PtRu@Cu-MOF}\)-based \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensor. The formation of a Schottky junction
increased the resistance of \(\mathrm{PtRu@Cu-MOF}\).[90] Among them, Cu-MOF adsorbed the exposed \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) and subsequently transferred electrons; the electron-transfer ability was enhanced after the introduction of Ru and Pt (Figure 14b). Ru also adsorbed \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), and Pt induced the transfer of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) to further enhance the response (Figure 14c). Owing to their similar mechanisms, \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}\) also detected \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) owing to electron transfer between \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) and the metal \((\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{Pt})\).[191] Generally, the sensitivity of sensors can be improved by adding a signal-amplifying circuit, whereas selectivity almost entirely depends on the characteristics of the sensing materials. Selectivity is the main index for evaluating the sensing performance of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\). For example, Kim and co-workers synthesized \(\mathrm{ZnO@ZIF-8}\), in which a network bridge structure promoted the signal transmission of the electrode plate (Figure 15a).[192] Compared to pure \(\mathrm{ZnO}\), \(\mathrm{ZnO@ZIF-8}\) had a more unique, selective, and dynamic resistance response to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) when subjected to the mixed reductive gases \(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{C}_{7}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) owing to the size-screening effect of ZIF-8 (Figure 15b). The selectivity of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) composite sensors is affected by the sieving characteristics of the MOF, and the functional sites in the M/MC and MOF regulate the interactive forces with molecules to achieve selective detection for different molecules. \(\mathrm{GaOOH@UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) simultaneously and selectively detected four heavy-metal ions– \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\). UiO-66-NH and \(\mathrm{GaOOH}\) (gallium oxide hydroxide) had abundant oxygen and nitrogen sites that could combine with the detected metal ions. The peak position and intensity of the four different peak currents did not interfere with each other, and the simultaneous detection of \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) was achieved.[193]
Under the action of current, certain detected substances may react with \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) on the electrode, and the identification then relies on the chemical reaction on the electrode A \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O@ZIF-67}\) electrochemical sensor was used to detect nonenzymatic glucose, with \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) enhancing the content of \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) in ZIF-67, and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) readily converting to \(\mathrm{Co}^{4+}\), which facilitated the electrooxidation of glucose (Figure 15c).[194] \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O@ZIF-67}\) exhibited higher electrooxidation ability for glucose than that of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and ZIF-67; therefore, it could be used as a highly selective and sensitive current response sensor. Gan and coworkers prepared \(\mathrm{Au@ZIF-8@GO}\) to electrocatalytically detect pesticides.[195] ZIF-8 provided a high specific surface area, and \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) acted as an auxiliary site of the oxidation-associated active center \(\mathrm{Au}\), increasing the electrocatalytic activity and durability in an actual sample.
Fluorescent sensors are typically simpler and faster than electrochemical detectors. For example, in the \(\mathrm{Cu@MOF-5}\) composite, MOF-5 limited the vibration of the surface groups on \(\mathrm{Cu}\), which hampered the nonradiative transition.[196] Therefore, the composite showed enhanced fluorescence emission and was applied as a pH sensor. As dispersive carriers, MOFs inhibit the aggregated transition of M/MCs from the distributed state and limit the loss of nanomaterials in solution owing to their restraining effect. The \(\mathrm{AuNCs@ZIF-8}\) fluorescent sensor rapidly detected bilirubin (BR).[197] ZIF-8 dispersed \(\mathrm{AuNCs}\) and led to a nearly sevenfold increase in the quantum efficiency of \(\mathrm{AuNC@ZIF-8}\). When BR approached ZIF-8 to compound with the \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) ions of the MOF, Au was released from \(\mathrm{AuNCs@ZIF-8}\), and the fluorescence intensity of the composite decreased rapidly (Figure 16a).
The fluorescence sensing applications of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) are being gradually explored. The single recognition sensor is gradually developing to multifunctional detection sensor, such as the continuous sensor of multi-materials. Liu and co-workers designed a \(\mathrm{Gd}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{:}\mathrm{Yb},\mathrm{Er}@\mathrm{UiO}\mathrm{-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) upconversion composite to realize continuous sensing for MB and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\).[60] The fluorescence emission spectrum of \(\mathrm{Gd}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{:}\mathrm{Yb},\mathrm{Er}@\mathrm{UiO}\mathrm{-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) coincided with the fluorescence absorption spectrum of MB; thus, the composite selectively detected MB molecules through the inner filtration effect. Subsequently, the MB molecules combined with the \(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet-}\) generated by \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (the Fenton reagent) and recovered the fluorescence of the composite, which completed the identification of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) after the MB molecule detection.
To further optimize the portability of fluorescence sensors, intricate instrument-free fluorescence sensing platforms such as sensing papers and visual-detection-based colorimetric cards are being studied. For example, \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{UiO}\mathrm{-MOF}\) was coated onto filter paper to prepare a portable sensing paper.[198] In this system, UiO-MOF acted as a gas adsorber and provided reactant aldehydes (formaldehyde, acetaldehyde, and acrolein) to \(\mathrm{ZnO}\), which then reacted with the aldehydes and imparted charge to \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\). Consequently, under aldehyde fumigation, the intensity ratio of the dual-emission peaks \(\left(I_{614}/I_{470}\right)\) for the composite increased and the color of the sensing paper changed from blue to red (Figure 16b). This change was utilized to achieve real-time, rapid, and selective detection of volatile aldehydes in mixed gases at different temperatures (formaldehyde, acetaldehyde, and acraldehyde detection limits: 42, 58, and \(66~\mathrm{ppb}\), respectively); moreover, the volume concentration was determined by calculating specific \(I_{614}/I_{470}\) ratios. In comparison, visual-detection-based colorimetric cards—which can visually reveal the estimated concentration of the measured substances—are more convenient to fabricate. For example, \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}\) was coated onto blank paper to prepare a simple, portable visual detection card to determine the concentration of the explosive triacetone triperoxide (TATP; Figure 16c).[199] Under acidic conditions, TATP decomposed into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}\) promoted the oxidation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for the other reactant—3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB)—forming a blue product oxTMB. Therefore, assembling \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{ZnMOF}\) and TMB on blank paper tape yielded a TATP-detecting test card, with the amount of the blue oxTMB product acting as the direct indicator of the reaction. By comparing the standard color card of oxTMB and the actual blue shade of oxTMB, the concentration of the TATP in water was preliminarily determined. This type of recognition/detection scheme based on visible color changes provides a new idea for designing portable small-molecule sensors.
Apart from common electrochemical and fluorescence sensors, partial \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) may help in constructing unusual sensors such as spectral detectors. Farha and co-workers introduced AuNRs exhibiting surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) into NU-901.[61] When \(\mathrm{AuNR}@\mathrm{NU}\mathrm{-}901\) was immersed in a \(4^{\prime}\)-mercaptobiphenylcarbonitrile (BTPCN) solution, the \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interactions between BTPCN and the porphyrin ring on the MOF helped embed BTPCN into the NU-901 cavity and subsequently move it to the AuNR surface; finally, the plasma surface enhancement effect of BTPCN was captured successfully. When the composite was immersed in a mixed solution of polystyrene
and BTPCN, only the characteristic spectrum of BTPCN was observed rather than that of PST-SH, owing to the screening effect of NU-901; therefore, \(\mathrm{AuNR}@\mathrm{NU}\mathrm{-}901\) selectively detected BTPCN. Additionally, a unique change in the Raman peaks was observed immediately after adding BTPCN in situ to an aqueous solution of \(\mathrm{AuNR}@\mathrm{NU}\mathrm{-}901\), indicating that \(\mathrm{AuNR}@\mathrm{NU}\mathrm{-}901\) directly tracked and recognized the diffusion and combination of BTPCN in the MOF through SERS.
In summary, the identification processes of different types of sensors typically depend on the response of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) in diverse media, or are linked to the chemical reactions between the composites and identified substances, in which the features of the composites are altered by the chemical reactions. The screening ability of MOFs and the recognition features of \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) are critical to the outstanding sensing performance of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\). The detected molecules can pass through the MOF pores and remain on the \(\mathrm{M/MC}\) surface for recognition. The \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\)-based embedded sensing platform is a typical example in which the intrinsic MOF cavities selectively provide transfer paths for specific molecules.
## 3.5. Absorption and Adsorption
Absorption is a process through which a substance absorbs other materials or energy. In terms of the study of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\), the field of electromagnetic absorption is worth targeting.[200] Good impedance matching and absorption capacity are the important criteria for realizing efficient electromagnetic-wave-absorbing \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\). The size adjustability and decent magnetic properties of \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) endow them with a specific electromagnetic absorption potential.[201,202] Moreover, the MOF-M/MC interface can generate interface dipoles and interfacial relaxation polarization, which promote polarization relaxation.[203] Therefore, \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) show promise for electromagnetic absorption. Recently, Liu et al. integrated \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and Co-MOF to prepare a composite, whose constituents had optimally matched electromagnetic absorption properties and enhanced electromagnetic absorption.[204] \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) increased the electromagnetic absorption ability, whereas Co-MOF exhibited dielectric loss owing to the enhanced interfacial polarization of the hierarchical structure. Moreover, the combination of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and Co-MOF led to enhanced interfacial polarization; therefore, the optimal reflection loss was 56.1 dB and the maximum bandwidth was 7.9 GHz at 2.0 mm. Essentially, efficient absorption and wide absorption bandwidths are key to designing electromagnetic-wave-absorbing \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\).
Adsorption is based on interfacial interactions, in which the adsorbed substances can adhere and accumulate on the adsorbent surface. This process mainly involves mass transfer, which is often used to separate pollutants in solution. \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) have significant application potential in adsorption-based processes. The adsorption effect mainly depends on the interaction between \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) and the adsorbate, which are affected by the active sites of \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\); the pore size, and functional groups of MOFs; and the MOF–M/MC interface.
In practical adsorption-related applications of the composites, external forces such as heat, pressure, and mechanical oscillations are typically used to accelerate the adsorption.[205]
\(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) adsorbents generally undergo adsorption–desorption–drying–recycling, which may easily lead to adsorbent loss; therefore, the fundamental problem in this context is the recyclability of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) adsorbents. Magnetic M/MCs are generally introduced into MOFs to enhance the recovery and reduce the postadsorption material loss.[206,207] The most prominent advantage of magnetic \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) is the improvement in the recyclability of MOFs in heterogeneous systems (Figure 17a).[208,209] For example, the function of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zr-MOF}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{TMU-17-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{CuNi-BTC}\) enhanced the recyclability of the composites.[205,210,211]
The action forces (including hydrogen bonding, chelation, and van der Waals interactions) and size matching between the \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) and adsorbates affect the adsorption efficiency and capacity of the composites. By designing the functional sites on MOFs, the \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) –adsorbate interactions can be altered to reasonably regulate the adsorptive effect for the adsorbed molecules. For example, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO-66}(\mathrm{MFC-O})\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}(\mathrm{MFC-N})\), and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO-66-urea}\) (MFC-U) exhibited distinct forces for metal ions and organic dyes.[212] As shown in Figure \(17\mathrm{b-d}\), during the adsorption of different ions by these composites, the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group on MFC-N exhibited strong interactions with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\), leading to an optimal adsorptive effect. The adsorption capacity of the composites for the organic dyes MO and MB mainly depended on electrostatic interactions. MFC-N had a lower negative zeta potential at high \(\mathrm{pH}\), which allowed MFC-N to exhibit high electrostatic attraction with cationic MB; therefore, MFC-N had the highest MB adsorption capacity. In contrast, MFC-N exhibited the weakest adsorption capacity for anionic MO owing to the electrostatic repulsion of the composite and MO. Obviously, electrostatic interaction also critically affects the adsorption. Zhou and co-workers synthesized positively charged \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\), whose adsorption performance was approximately 1.7 times that of \(\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\).[213] The \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) in the MOF was partially replaced by \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\) during the combination with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), enabling the framework to bear more positive charges for electrostatically attracting the negatively charged MO. In fact, the interactions between \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) and the adsorbed pollutants are a coexistence of multiple forces. Guo and co-workers synthesized heterogeneous \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Zn-MOF-74}\), which could simultaneously remove \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) and TC.[214] The composite exhibited electrostatic interactions and \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking between \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) and TC, and the pollutant molecule underwent ion exchange with the guest of the MOF, further promoting the adsorptive effect. Surprisingly, \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) and TC complexed with \(\mathrm{Zn-MOF-74}\) and formed \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Zn-MOF-74-}\) Cu-TC on the \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Zn-MOF-74}\) surface, further enhancing the adsorption capacity of the composite.
trostatic repulsion of the composite and MO. Obviously, electrostatic interaction also critically affects the adsorption. Zhou and co-workers synthesized positively charged \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\) , whose adsorption performance was approximately 1.7 times that of \(\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\) .[213] The \(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}\) in the MOF was partially replaced by \(\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}\) during the combination with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) , enabling the framework to bear more positive charges for electrostatically attracting the negatively charged MO. In fact, the interactions between \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) and the adsorbed pollutants are a coexistence of multiple forces. Guo and co-workers synthesized heterogenous \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Zn-MOF-74}\) , which could simultaneously remove \(C\mathbf{u}^{2+}\) and TC.[214] The composite exhibited electrostatic interactions and \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking between \(C\mathbf{u}^{2+}\) and TC, and the pollutant molecule underwent ion exchange with the guest of the MOF, further promoting the adsorptive effect. Surprisingly, \(C\mathbf{u}^{2+}\) and TC complexed with Zn-MOF-74 and formed \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Zn-MOF-74-}\) Cu-TC on the \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Zn-MOF-74}\) surface, further enhancing the adsorption capacity of the composite.
The aforementioned discussion suggests that \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) are more widely used for adsorption than for absorption. The absorption-based applications of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) are targeted toward electromagnetic waves. However, the limited magnetic and dielectric losses of many MOFs limit the development of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\). The composites are usually processed as precursor materials to generate \(\mathrm{M/MC}\)-doped \(\mathrm{C/N}\)-based microwave-absorbing materials and to improve magnetic–dielectric synergistic processes.[215] Nevertheless, we still believe that Nevertheless, designing the surface charges and polar groups of MOFs could viably regulate the polarization relaxation to enhance the dielectric loss of MOFs, thus expanding the
electromagnetic-absorption-related applications of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\) MOFs. In comparison, the adsorption-related applications of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\) MOFs are far more numerous, because the adsorption effect of the composites depends on the composite–adsorbate interactions. These interplays can be adequately controlled by designing the inherent characteristics of MOFs (such as structure, size, and group) and the oriented functionality of \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) (using magnetic materials, for instance). The straightforward adjustment of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\) MOFs establishes an adequate foundation for adsorption-related applications.
## 3.6. Supercapacitors
The application potential of MOFs for supercapacitors was first confirmed in 2011 through experiments on the energy density, specific capacitance, and electrochemical cycling life of two Co-based MOFs.[216,217] The capacitive properties of MOFs have gradually been explored since then.[218] Factors such as the specific surface area, pore size distribution, and conductivity are related to the electrochemical behavior of MOFs, with poor conductivity predominantly impeding the development of capacitive MOFs in recent years.[219] \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) have numerous redox-active sites and good conductivity; certain metallic oxides have been proven to exhibit outstanding capacitance properties, whereas other \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) are gradually being studied. Although the high activity of \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) is instrumental to enhancing the electrochemical performance, extreme activities cause excessive reactivity-induced loss of electrode materials and lead to a short capacitance life. MOFs can protect \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) and complement their electrochemical weaknesses, enabling \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\) MOFs to function as highly stable supercapacitive materials. The cooperative \(\mathrm{MOF-M/MC}\) interactions can also promote the conductivity of MOFs. Therefore, \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\) MOF composites can function as electrode materials with high specific capacitance and ultralong electrochemical life. Pang and coworkers assembled a \(\delta\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) composite film with a high potential window, which had a battery capacity of \(667\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) in a three-electrode system.[220] \(\delta\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) was also combined with activated carbon to prepare a double-electrode asymmetric battery material that had an ultrahigh specific capacity of \(340\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). During charge and discharge, the composite film either inhibited electrolysis or prevented electron conduction and permitted ion conduction.
Close interfacial connections between MOFs and \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) can promote the capacitance of the composites, because distinct interfacial defects could restrain the MOF-M/MC synergy, affecting the maximum specific capacitance of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\) MOFs. Liu and coworkers prepared \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) on copper foam, which was then used to produce \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2+1}\mathrm{O}\) nanorods in a \(\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) precursor solution to form \(\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) with a fixed orientation on the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2+1}\mathrm{O}\) surface.[221] Under the assisted induction of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), highly oriented \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Cu}_{2+1}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) with robust connections was prepared (Figure 18a). \(\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) had high electric double-layer capacitance, and the effective battery capacitor—\(\mathrm{Cu}_{2+1}\mathrm{O}\)—enhanced the conductivity and interfacial faradaic process of the composite. Consequently, the \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Cu}_{2+1}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) supercapacitor exhibited a high energy density \((25.67\;\mathrm{Wh}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\) and ultrahigh specific capacitance \((518.58\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\); Figure 18b). Additionally, the device manufactured using \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Cu}_{2+1}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cu-MOF}\) could briefly charge a
mobile phone or an LED light (Figure 18c). The amount of the \(\mathrm{M/MC}\) in the MOF can affect the capacitive performance of the composites. In \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\), the electric current gradually increased with increasing \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) content, whereas the electrochemical activity began to decrease at Ce dopant concentrations greater than \(0.5\times10^{-3}\;\mathrm{M}\), because high concentrations of \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) could not be adequately dispersed by the MOF.[222] The \(0.5\times10^{-3}\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) dopant achieved optimal dispersion in ZIF-8, which exposed more active sites and promoted the migration of electrolytic ions. The maximum capacitance of the \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8{-}0.5\) electrode in a three-electrode system reached \(424.2\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and the capacitance of the electrode-incorporated symmetric supercapacitor cell with the composite was \(89\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}@1.0\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The thickness of the MOF also affects the electrochemical performance of the composites. Pang and co-workers synthesized \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\) Mn-MOF-x with different Mn-MOF thicknesses. Mn-HHTP was grown vertically on the (0010) surface of \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\), and the ion-transporting channel was oriented along the [001] direction.[223] As the MOF gradually grew and thickened on the (0010) plane of \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\), the mass transfer path increased. However, the extremely thick MOF significantly lengthened the mass transfer distance; therefore, \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}@\) Mn-MOF-4 exhibited optimal specific capacitance \((408.7\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}@1.0\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). Subsequently, the composite and activated carbon were assembled as an aqueous asymmetric supercapacitor, which exhibited a specific capacitance of \(231.3\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and a coulombic efficiency of \(96.6\%\). In summary, the supercapacitive performance of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\) MOF composites is affected by factors including the robustness of the connections and the loading amount. The current studies have been provided useful reference data for optimizing the electrochemical properties of \(\mathrm{M/MC}@\) MOFs.
Therefore, the integration of \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) and MOFs leads to bidirectional promotive effects. MOFs overcome the disadvantages of pure \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) such as high reaction loss and short capacitor lifetimes, whereas \(\mathrm{M/MCs}\) improve the poor conductivity of independent MOFs. Additionally, the ordered porous structure of MOFs facilitates selective transport for electrolyte ions. Moreover, the robust heterogeneous interface can not only promote
the MOF-M/MC synergy to enhance the ion conduction, but also increases the stability of the composites during charging and discharging. These properties of M/MC@MOFs can be exploited for supercapacitor-related applications.
## 3.7. Other
Photosensitive M/MC@MOFs have application potential in other arenas such as photodynamic therapy and antibacterial action. For example, MOFs can act as carriers and preserve the stability of M/MCs in organisms. When M/MC@MOFs reach specific microenvironments, the encapsulated photosensitive M/MC can be released through the controllable collapse of the MOF to achieve optimal efficacy. The acidic tumor microenvironment destroys MOFs to release M/MCs such as AuNCs, which can produce abundant singlet oxygen under laser irradiation and inhibit tumor activity.[224] Additionally, MOF carriers can control the size of photosensitive M/MCs and their interactions to enhance the performance of M/MC@MOFs. Jiang and co-workers assembled AuNCs@ZIF-8 exhibiting antibacterial properties.[225] AuNCs@ZIF-8 showed higher fluorescence than that of AuNCs and ZIF-8 owing to the metal dispersion of the MOF. Under white light, AuNCs@ZIF-8 completely inactivated Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus cells. Specifically, ZIF-8 activated oxygen molecules through internal catalysis, and AuNCs promoted the generation of active oxygen, which could be transported to bacteria through the microporous structure, thus achieving sterilization; moreover, the ZIF-8-induced size control of the AuNCs further enhanced the antibacterial activity.
## 3.8. Relationship Elucidation of Multiple Applications
M/MC@MOFs are multifunctional materials that could concurrently exhibit outstanding adsorption, luminescence, catalytic, and electrochemical properties. Typically, these functions are not completely independent of each other. While performing a certain activity, other functions may collaborate.
1) Adsorption-catalysis: Adsorption is a crucial step in catalytic reactions. Chemical adsorption is an important indicator of the catalytic effects. Chemical adsorption is selective and can accumulate reactants on the catalyst surface through interactive forces, which can reduce the activation energy of the reaction. Note that a low adsorption force leads to a low reaction rate, whereas an extremely strong adsorption force will lead to the difficult breaking of the adsorbents and reactants, thereby affecting the catalytic activity. The promoting effect of MOFs on catalytic reactions largely depends on their moderate adsorption capacity for reactants, which facilitates the transport of reactants to the surface of the catalytically active M/MC centers without affecting the interactions and reaction between the M/MC and reactants. 2) Photoinduction-adsorption/desorption: Under light induction, MOFs containing electropositive and electronegative groups may undergo intramolecular charge transfer, and the local charge density can be modified.[226] The changes in charge distribution can affect the adsorption force. Besides,
if there are isomerized light-induced molecules in MOF cavities, it is possible to cause cis–trans isomerization of the molecule under light induction, which may affect the spatial hindrance of MOF cavities. Therefore, the light illumination may regulate adsorption or desorption by affecting the charge distribution and steric hindrance of materials.
3) Adsorption-sensing: In general, the selective detecting ability of M/MC@MOFs involves the MOF selectively adsorbing and transporting specific substances to the M/MC active center, followed by completion of the detection through changes in characteristics (under light, electricity, and magnetism). Therefore, the adsorption characteristic directly affects the sensing performance of materials.
## 4. Conclusions and Perspectives
M/MC@MOF composites are typical multicomponent materials that can be synthesized using liquid-, solid-, and gas-phase methods, through which M/MCs are introduced into MOFs. Liquid-phase synthesis, which is the most popular method, is convenient, easy to operate, and can permit the effective capture of specific active components that are more compatible with MOFs. Solid-state synthesis methods are typically green in nature and are not applied extensively owing to the low reactivity of M/MC@MOFs in the solid state. Gas-phase synthesis methods can aid in precisely designing the number, thickness, and deposition state of each composite layer; however, their applications are also limited owing to the harsh reaction conditions and complex reaction steps. M/MC@MOFs integrate the functions of M/MCs and MOFs to obtain excellent performance in traditional catalysis, photocatalysis, electrochemistry, sensing, adsorption, and supercapacitance.
The development-related dilemmas of M/MC@MOFs deserve attention. For example, ensuring a precise distribution ratio of the heterogeneous M/MC and MOF in composites is challenging. Moreover, during the synthesis of M/MC@MOFs, the interfacial boundary and dynamic control of the interfacial defects are noteworthy difficulties. Additionally, the interactions between the internal M/MC and external MOF are limited to weak forces such as electrostatic interactions, van der Waals forces, and hydrogen bonding, which are insufficient for prolonged preservation of the composites. Over the long term, the weak interfacial interactions can affect the robust connections between the functional M/MC and MOFs, and affect the stability of the electron/energy transfer. Therefore, attempts should be made to use stronger covalent bonds to connect the internal M/MC and external MOF, which will significantly improve the initial activity and cycling life of M/MC@MOFs.
Additionally, the production of multifunctional MOF composites should be targeted to broaden the applications of MOF composites. Simultaneous assembly of multiple M/MCs and MOFs should be primarily focused on for realizing new heterogeneous M/MC@MOF composites, such as (A+B+...)@MOFs, A@B@MOFs, MOFs@(A, B, A+B, or more)@MOFs, and (A, B, A+B, or more)@MOF@MOFs (where A, B... represent different types of M/MCs incorporated into the MOFs). Existing synthesis routes can aid in synthesizing these multifunctional M/MC@MOFs, thereby providing unlimited possibilities for exploring the performance of these multifunctional composites
under the influence of forces such as light, electricity, and magnetism.
In summary, recently published \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOF}\) composites were reviewed in this article. Existing conventional synthesis technologies and emerging methods are both expected to establish a foundation for advancing the development of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\). More breakthroughs can be achieved in terms of the properties of \(\mathrm{M/MC@MOFs}\) with improvements in the synthesis approaches. MOFs remain the optimal choice for developing high-mechanical-strength enclosures and multifunctional composites. Additionally, more functional MOF materials that can be upgraded from the laboratory scale to industrial levels should be developed.
